window.INFO={"id":"grass","title":"The Queensland Grass Rush, 1840 to 1902","subtitle":"An information bank about squatters, runs and stations, sheep and cattle, land laws and drought","description":"A non-fiction information bank about the Queensland grass rush: squatters, runs and stations, the move from sheep to cattle, land laws, drought and Aboriginal people, 1840 to 1902. Overview, timeline, places, people, real lives and sources. It also covers the money from London and Scotland behind the runs, and a map of how settlement spread by decade.","dateLeft":"Queensland · 1840 to 1902","dateRight":"By Robin McKean","theme":"#1f2b1a","footnote":"Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander readers are advised that this resource names places where people died. Everything in it comes from the sources listed on the Sources page.","footer":"The Queensland Grass Rush. A non-fiction information bank by Robin McKean. This resource has no tracking and no cookies. An earlier choose-your-path story version is kept in the archive folder of the project on GitHub.","warning":["This resource is about grassland that Aboriginal people had cared for over thousands of years, and about the squatters who took it up for sheep and cattle. It says that violence against Aboriginal people and violence against settlers happened. It does not describe the violence in detail.","Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander readers are advised that this resource names places where people died, and that the old records it draws on were written by Europeans."],"review":"Review status: this resource has not yet been reviewed by Aboriginal community members. Review is being sought. Please tell the author if anything needs to change.","sourcesIntro":"Every fact in this resource comes from one of these sources. Newspapers named in the sources can be read on Trove. Where a newspaper report is quoted by a researcher or a blog, that page is listed, not the newspaper. The Folklore and legends page also uses some sources of this kind. They are listed here with the others, and the stories are labelled as unproven.","imageNote":"All images are old photographs, maps or drawings that are in the public domain, and each is credited. No images in this resource were made by artificial intelligence. Some photographs show groups of workers. The sources read here do not say who they were, so the captions do not say either.","overview":{"image":"yandilla","lead":"From 1840 squatters took up grassland in what is now Queensland, first on the Darling Downs and then further north and west. They grazed sheep and later cattle on large runs. The land was Aboriginal Country, managed for thousands of years. Conflict, land laws, drought and the market for wool and beef shaped the next sixty years.","glance":[{"n":"92","label":"conflict events counted on the Darling Downs by Wallis and Burke","src":["atf"]},{"n":"3,000 km²","label":"the size of some stations at the height of squatter pastoralism in the 1880s","src":["nma"]},{"n":"106 to 54 million","label":"sheep in Australia in 1892 and in 1903, after the Federation Drought","src":["nma"]},{"n":"Nearly 3 million","label":"cattle lost in Queensland alone in the Federation Drought","src":["nma"]},{"n":"35.6% to 91.6%","label":"bank advances in Queensland as a share of the colony’s output, 1880 and 1892","src":["merrett"]}],"sections":[{"h":"Squatters, runs and stations","p":["A “squatter” was a person who grazed stock on Crown land, at first without a lease. A “run” was the area of land a squatter held. A “station” was the run together with its buildings and workers. In 1842 the northern districts of New South Wales, which are now south-east Queensland, were opened to free settlement. The Leslie brothers took up runs on the Darling Downs from March 1840, and the Downs was officially opened for settlement in 1843.","The State Library of Queensland says the first squatters came to sell good raw wool to English mills, and that many were younger sons of English and Scottish gentry. The squatters ruled over a labour force of travelling bush workers, stockmen and shepherds. The State Library says the squatters’ way of life on the Darling Downs lasted less than fifty years."],"src":["atf","slqShepherd","tales"]},{"h":"Aboriginal people and the grass","p":["The Downs had been the home, for thousands of years, of the Keinjan, Giabal, Jarowair and Barunggam peoples. They practised a burning season to manage the grasses of the plains. Archaeologists Lynley Wallis and Heather Burke write that the grasslands were vital to Aboriginal people and were managed by annual cultural burning.","Wallis and Burke describe the period from 1840 to the end of the 1850s on the Downs as almost continuous conflict, and say about 90 per cent of it took place on pastoral runs. Settlers also killed Aboriginal people, in parties led by squatters and in single attacks. By the 1850s Aboriginal defiance had worn down in the southern Downs."],"src":["slqShepherd","atf"]},{"h":"The Native Police and the frontier","p":["The Native Police set up their first camp in what became Queensland in May 1849, at Callandoon. In 1851 squatters built barracks near the present Condamine, at their own expense, after asking for help. In 1858 a squatter told a New South Wales committee that his district became quiet within three months of the Native Police arriving, and that his land became fifty per cent more valuable.","In October 1861, near Springsure in central Queensland, Gayiri people killed 19 members of Horatio Wills’s party at Cullin-la-ringo. Afterwards, settlers and then Native Police killed many Gayiri people. The exact number is not known."],"src":["atf","newcastle","guardian"]},{"h":"Land laws","p":["Queensland became a separate colony on 10 December 1859. The Crown Lands Alienation Act of 1868 opened land that was not under a pastoral lease to selection. The same Act let pastoralists make pre-emptive selections from their own leases, and the Queensland State Archives says this let pastoralists secure land along creek and river banks. The Lands Act of 1884 abolished pre-emptive purchases.","The National Museum of Australia says the eastern colonies passed land laws in the 1860s to control squatters and encourage closer settlement, with the aim of breaking large runs into smaller blocks for farming and grazing. It says this was not always achieved, because squatters found ways to hold on to productive country."],"src":["abs150","qsaLand","nma"]},{"h":"From sheep to cattle","p":["Gracemere was a sheep station run by the Archer family near Rockhampton. Black spear grass overran the country, and the station’s wool book described more and more bales as “seedy”. The family bought country at Tambo in 1872 and drove the sheep there between 1872 and 1874. By 1880 there were no sheep left at Gracemere.","The Ross River Meatworks at Townsville began to be built in August 1891, and by June 1892 the first cattle were being processed. The first shipment of frozen beef, 600 tons, left Townsville for London."],"src":["fitzroy","ross"]},{"h":"Money from London","p":["The runs were stocked and fenced with borrowed money, and some of it came from banks and companies in London and Scotland or from British savers. A wool lien let a squatter pay a lender from the next wool clip. The “Money from London” page names the lenders, checks how much each was really in Queensland, and follows the money through the financial crisis of 1866, the land boom of the 1880s, the bank failures of 1893 and the Federation Drought.","The Bank of New South Wales opened the first bank in Brisbane in 1850, and the Union Bank followed in 1858. The Scottish Australian Investment Company financed Bowen Downs, which received its first cattle in 1862. Dalgety, in contrast, did not open in Queensland until 1891."],"src":["slqBNSW","unionWiki","jcuBowen","anuDalgety"]},{"h":"Strikes, drought and the end of the squatters’ era","p":["In 1891 central Queensland was occupied for almost four months with a strike by shearers. The strike failed, and thirteen union leaders were sentenced to three years in prison on 20 May 1891.","The National Museum of Australia says the Federation Drought, from 1895 to 1903, was the worst in Australia’s history, measured by stock losses. Banks foreclosed on some stations and many graziers walked off their land. The Museum says the drought ended squatter-dominated pastoralism in New South Wales and Queensland, as bank foreclosures and the resumption of leases led to the partition of large stations."],"src":["slqStrike","nma"]}],"words":[["Squatter","A person who grazed stock on Crown land, at first without a lease."],["Run","The area of land a squatter held."],["Station","The run together with its buildings and workers."],["Head station","The main set of buildings on a run."],["Outstation","A hut and yards a long way from the head station."],["Shepherd","A worker who watched a flock of sheep."],["Hut keeper","A worker who looked after a hut and its yards."],["Selector","A person who took up a block of land under a Land Act."],["Lease","The right to use Crown land for a set time."],["Pre-emptive purchase","The right of a pastoralist to buy land from his own lease."],["Resumption","The government taking back part of a lease."],["Dummying","Selecting land on behalf of someone else, against the law. (This meaning is explained from general knowledge. The Archives page read does not define it.)"],["Merino","A breed of sheep with fine wool."],["Dispersal","The word used in the records for breaking up an Aboriginal camp by force."],["Lien","A claim a lender has on something, such as the next wool clip, so the lender is paid from it first."],["Stock mortgage","A loan in which animals are the security. The lender can claim them if the loan is not repaid."],["Mortgagee","The lender who holds a mortgage."],["Foreclose","To take property because a loan was not repaid."],["Debenture","A written promise to repay a loan with interest. Companies sold them to savers."],["Agency house","A firm that sold a squatter’s wool and supplied goods, and often lent money. It is also called a stock and station agent."],["Pastoral finance company","A company that lent money to pastoralists, often money it had collected from savers or borrowed from banks."],["Suspend payment","When a bank stops paying depositors on demand."],["Reconstruction","A plan to reopen a failed bank on new terms, with depositors waiting longer for their money."],["Gazetted","Announced officially in the Government Gazette. A district was gazetted when the Government named it. Settlers could arrive before or after that date."]],"left":["This resource does not describe violence in detail. It gives counts and dates from the sources.","It gives no names or customs of Aboriginal people beyond what the sources say. The old records often use insulting words about Aboriginal people, and some of the web pages named in the sources quote them. Those words are not copied here.","No personal account by an Aboriginal person of the 1840s on the Downs was found in the sources read here. The records are almost all by squatters, officials and settlers."]},"timeline":[{"date":"March 1840","title":"The Leslie brothers take up runs on the Darling Downs","text":"The Leslie brothers took up runs from March 1840. The Downs had been the home, for thousands of years, of the Keinjan, Giabal, Jarowair and Barunggam peoples, who practised a burning season to manage the grasses.","src":["atf","slqShepherd"]},{"date":"March 1841","title":"Fifty-five European men on the Downs","text":"A census recorded 55 European men on the Downs.","src":["atf"]},{"date":"15 July 1841","title":"Cressbrook: the first run in the Brisbane Valley","text":"David McConnel took up Cressbrook, widely credited as the first run in the Brisbane Valley. Wallis and Burke say he had substantial family funding from England.","src":["atfMoreton"]},{"date":"November 1841","title":"A squatter’s letter about a reprisal","text":"George Leslie wrote to his parents that Aboriginal people had killed or carried away about 60 young ewes, and that the Leslies had “given them what they will not forget in a hurry”. Wallis and Burke read this as a major reprisal.","src":["atf"]},{"date":"1 February 1842","title":"Kilcoy station poisoning","text":"At Kilcoy station, flour mixed with strychnine was given to a large group of Aboriginal people by two shepherds employed by the lessee, Evan MacKenzie. At least sixty people died. Kilcoy is east of the Darling Downs, not on the Downs.","flag":"The record cites the Sydney Morning Herald of 5 December 1842. Kilcoy is included because of its link to the region, not because it is on the Downs.","src":["awKilcoy"]},{"date":"5 May 1842","title":"Moreton declared a pastoral district","text":"The Moreton district was declared open for free settlement. Within six years 18 runs had been taken up.","src":["atfMoreton"]},{"date":"1842","title":"Northern districts opened to free settlement","text":"Labour for the runs was recruited in Sydney, and recruits were offered a free fare and a bonus.","src":["tales"]},{"date":"January 1842 to November 1843","title":"Thirteen shepherds killed","text":"The Commissioner of Crown Lands, Christopher Rolleston, reported that at least 13 shepherds had been killed, and that thousands of sheep had been killed or driven off. The violence also raised wages.","src":["atf"]},{"date":"1843","title":"The first runs in Wide Bay and the Burnett","text":"John Eales established Noomoowoolloo at Tiaro in Wide Bay. Henry Stuart Russell and William Glover took up Burrandowan in the Burnett.","src":["atfWideBay"]},{"date":"1843","title":"The Downs opened for settlement","text":"The Downs was officially opened for settlement in 1843. A census in September 1843 recorded about 300 people, including a reasonable number of women. Rolleston became Commissioner of Crown Lands in 1843. In 1843 and 1844 Wallis and Burke describe a state of open warfare across the Downs.","src":["atf"]},{"date":"1846 to January 1847","title":"Wages and a sentence for refusing to go to a station","text":"The Moreton Bay Courier reported wages of £25 a year for good shepherds and £22 for hut keepers, with good rations. The paper also complained that some men took the fare and then stayed in Brisbane. In January 1847 it reported that a man who had been hired as a shepherd and refused to go to his station was sentenced to three months’ hard labour.","flag":"These newspaper reports are read as they are quoted in a blog, not on Trove.","src":["tales"]},{"date":"1848","title":"The year with the most conflict events on the Downs","text":"The year with the most events was 1848, with 16 events and 27 deaths: 20 Aboriginal people and 7 Europeans.","src":["atf"]},{"date":"Late 1840s","title":"The first runs in the Maranoa and the Leichhardt","text":"Allan MacPherson took up Mount Abundance in the Maranoa within a year of Mitchell’s expedition of 1846. Squatters took up Taroom and Juandah in the Leichhardt. The Maranoa was declared open in late 1848, as were the Burnett and Wide Bay.","flag":"Wallis and Burke’s table gives 1847 for Wide Bay, and their text says 1848.","src":["atfMaranoa","atfLeichhardt","atfWideBay","atfIndex"]},{"date":"26 October 1848","title":"Canning Creek","text":"Three Aboriginal women and a child were killed at Canning Creek. Four people saw it, but all said they could not name those who did it. Rolleston wrote that he could not trace them.","src":["atf"]},{"date":"May 1849","title":"The first Native Police camp in what became Queensland","text":"Commandant Frederick Walker set up the camp at Callandoon, on the present border, with 14 troopers recruited from the Murrumbidgee and Murray River areas of southern New South Wales and northern Victoria.","src":["atf"]},{"date":"November 1850","title":"The first bank opens in Brisbane","text":"The Bank of New South Wales opened a branch in Queen Street, Brisbane. The State Library of Queensland says traders had struggled to get a southern bank to open in the town. The bank was based in Sydney.","src":["slqBNSW"]},{"date":"1851","title":"Squatters build barracks","text":"After asking for help, squatters built barracks at Wandai Gumbal, near the present Condamine, at their own expense.","src":["atf"]},{"date":"November 1853","title":"A newspaper says the first fierce resistance is “subdued”","text":"Wallis and Burke cite a newspaper of November 1853 that said the first fierce resistance had been “subdued”, and they say violence was still occurring sporadically.","src":["atf"]},{"date":"10 January 1854","title":"Port Curtis and Leichhardt declared","text":"The Port Curtis and Leichhardt pastoral districts were proclaimed. Gladstone was the first permanent settlement in Port Curtis in 1854, and Rockhampton followed in 1856.","src":["atfPortCurtis","atfLeichhardt"]},{"date":"1858","title":"The Union Bank opens in Brisbane","text":"The Union Bank of Australia, founded in London in 1837, opened a Brisbane branch. In 1864 parliament asked it to place the Queensland Government’s loan.","src":["unionWiki","hodes1866"]},{"date":"1858","title":"A squatter’s evidence on the Native Police","text":"A squatter told a New South Wales committee that his district became quiet within three months of the Native Police arriving, and that his land became fifty per cent more valuable.","src":["atf"]},{"date":"10 December 1859","title":"Queensland becomes a separate colony","text":"Until then Queensland was part of New South Wales.","src":["abs150"]},{"date":"1860","title":"The Liens on Wool Act","text":"The first session of the Queensland Parliament passed the Liens on Wool Act. It let a squatter give a lender a claim on the wool clip, and it covered mortgages on sheep, cattle and horses.","src":["ofhc"]},{"date":"1861","title":"The first Kennedy runs and the Warrego","text":"Many first runs on the Burdekin in the Kennedy district date from 1861. Joseph Fleming took up more than 100,000 hectares in the Warrego in August 1861 and was declared bankrupt by November 1862.","src":["atfKennedy","atfWarrego"]},{"date":"17 October 1861","title":"Cullin-la-ringo","text":"Near Springsure, Gayiri people killed 19 members of Horatio Wills’s party. A University of Newcastle research database records that two Aboriginal boys had been taken by white men before the attack, and records one view that the attack was in reprisal for the killing of Gayiri people about a week earlier by Native Police. Afterwards, settlers and then Native Police killed many Gayiri people. The Guardian reports official records of at least 30 killed on the first day, and a newspaper report that Native Police shot 60 or 70 people.","flag":"The numbers of Aboriginal people killed are not known. The sources give figures from at least 30 to about 300, and some people at the time claimed it was far larger.","src":["newcastle","guardian"]},{"date":"Early 1862","title":"The first runs in the Mitchell district","text":"James Allan and Ernest Davis took up runs in the south-east of the Mitchell district.","src":["atfMitchell"]},{"date":"October 1862","title":"The Scottish Australian Investment Company finances Bowen Downs","text":"The first cattle arrived at Bowen Downs, on Iningai land, in October 1862. Wallis and Burke say the station had finance from the Scottish Australian Investment Company, and the James Cook University archive says the Scottish-Australian Company provided the capital.","src":["atfMitchell","jcuBowen","muttaburra"]},{"date":"1863 to 1865","title":"Two London-formed companies open in Brisbane","text":"The Bank of Queensland, set up in London, opened in Brisbane on 13 August 1863. The Australian Mercantile Land and Finance Company, registered in England on 5 November 1863, opened its first Australian office in Brisbane that year and moved its business to Victoria in 1865.","src":["boq","anuAMLF","qhAMLF"]},{"date":"1864 and 1865","title":"The Warrego and Burke districts gazetted","text":"The Warrego was gazetted in 1864 and Burke in 1865. In 1864 John Graham MacDonald brought stock to the Nicholson River and set up Floraville, near Burketown.","src":["atfIndex","atfBurke"]},{"date":"1866","title":"The first runs in the Gregory districts","text":"Buckingham Downs in Gregory North was taken up in 1866, and one account gives 1866 for Nockatunga in Gregory South.","flag":"Another account gives a licence of 1868 for the first occupation of Gregory South.","src":["atfGregoryN","atfGregoryS"]},{"date":"May to July 1866","title":"The 1866 financial crisis","text":"The London firm Overend, Gurney and Company closed on 10 May 1866. Agra and Masterman’s Bank, whose Sydney agents were to place £500,000 of a Queensland loan in London, was involved in the failure. The news reached Brisbane on 10 July. The Union Bank cancelled a promised £100,000 advance, the Bank of Queensland suspended payment, and parliament passed a bill for £300,000 of Treasury bills on 20 July.","flag":"Wikipedia says Agra and Masterman’s suspended payment on 6 June 1866. Hodes says it was “involved in” the failure and that it telegraphed on 22 July that it could carry out its agreement after all.","src":["hodes1866","agraWiki","boq"]},{"date":"1867 to 1872","title":"John Douglas loses Tooloombah","text":"John Douglas, later Premier, had borrowed £5,000 from the Sydney firm Gilchrist, Watt and Company to buy Tooloombah. In 1867 he was forced to transfer the station to the firm. The sale did not fully clear his debt, and he was insolvent in 1872.","src":["hodesDouglas"]},{"date":"1868","title":"Crown Lands Alienation Act","text":"The Act opened land not under a pastoral lease to selection. Selectors paid annual rent, and could freehold agricultural land after three years if they met the conditions. The same Act let pastoralists make pre-emptive selections from their own leases, at one acre for every ten shillings of improvements.","src":["qsaLand"]},{"date":"1869","title":"Pastoral Leases Act","text":"The Queensland State Archives says that much pastoral land leased under this Act was deserted in the drought. Archibald Archer wrote in 1972 that Gracemere was reduced by resumptions after the Land Acts of 1868 and 1869.","src":["qsaLand","fitzroy"]},{"date":"Late 1860s to 1871","title":"Spear grass at Gracemere","text":"In the late 1860s the station’s wool book described more and more bales as “seedy”. In 1871 the manager, James Archer, wrote that the paddocks, after all the expense they had been, were “very little good”. Wool was then nearly two shillings a pound.","src":["fitzroy"]},{"date":"1872","title":"The Queensland National Bank opens","text":"The Queensland National Bank was set up in Brisbane by Queensland businessmen, with Edward Drury as general manager. The bank was awarded all the Queensland Government’s business in 1879.","src":["wikiQNB","qldHeritageQNB","rba90"]},{"date":"1872 to 1874","title":"The Archers move their sheep to Tambo","text":"In 1872 the Archer family bought country at Tambo and called it Minnie Downs. The sheep were driven there from Gracemere between 1872 and 1874.","src":["fitzroy"]},{"date":"1873","title":"Gregory North and Gregory South gazetted","text":"The two Gregory districts were gazetted in 1873, though stock had reached Gregory North a decade earlier.","src":["atfIndex","atfGregoryN"]},{"date":"1876","title":"Lands Act on “dummying”","text":"The Act of 1876 tried to stop “dummying” by pastoralists, but the Queensland State Archives says those efforts were unsuccessful.","src":["qsaLand"]},{"date":"1877","title":"Jondaryan the largest freehold run in Queensland","text":"By 1877 the Jondaryan run on the Downs was the largest freehold run in Queensland, owned by Edward Wienholt and the trustees of William Kent.","src":["atf"]},{"date":"1880","title":"No sheep left at Gracemere","text":"When Robert Stubbs Archer became manager in 1880, there were no sheep left at Gracemere.","src":["fitzroy"]},{"date":"1880s","title":"The height of squatter-dominated pastoralism","text":"Stations in New South Wales and Queensland were sometimes up to 3,000 square kilometres in size and carried hundreds of thousands of merino sheep.","src":["nma"]},{"date":"1884","title":"Two more agency companies","text":"The New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency Company opened branches in Brisbane and Rockhampton. Dalgety and Company Limited was registered in London on 29 April 1884, but its first Queensland branch opened in 1891.","src":["anuNZLM","anuDalgety"]},{"date":"1884","title":"Lands Act","text":"The Lands Act of 1884 abolished pre-emptive purchases. Up to 1,280 acres could be selected as an Agricultural Farm and freeholded after five years, and up to 20,000 acres could be selected as a Grazing Farm and held on a 30-year lease.","src":["qsaLand"]},{"date":"1885 and 1886","title":"The land is fully claimed and the railway reaches Barcaldine","text":"By 1885 all the available land in Gregory South had been claimed. The railway reached Barcaldine in 1886 and Longreach in 1892.","src":["atfGregoryS","muttaburra"]},{"date":"1885 to 1887","title":"First large resumptions at Bowen Downs","text":"The local history says the first large land resumptions at Bowen Downs were in 1885 to 1887, in a decade of droughts and falling wool prices.","src":["muttaburra"]},{"date":"1880s to 1888","title":"The land boom","text":"The 1909 Government review says property prices doubled or quadrupled in months in the 1880s, and that demand almost stopped in the second half of 1888. Merrett says Queensland’s bank advances grew from 35.6 per cent of the colony’s output in 1880 to 91.6 per cent in 1892. Goldsbrough Mort and Company was formed in 1888.","src":["ofhc","merrett","anuGM"]},{"date":"April 1889 to December 1890","title":"The employers organise","text":"The Queensland Pastoral Employers’ Association formed at Barcaldine in April 1889. Pastoralists met in Melbourne and Sydney in November and December 1890 to form the Pastoralists’ Union of Australia.","src":["woodley"]},{"date":"June 1890","title":"Goldsbrough Mort’s Rockhampton manager asks for more money","text":"A catalogue entry lists a letter from the company’s Rockhampton manager, in June 1890, asking for further advances to continue business. Only the entry was read, not the letter.","src":["anuGM"]},{"date":"February to May 1891","title":"The shearers’ strike","text":"Shearers refused to work after pastoralists proposed to cut their wages, which were then £1 for every hundred sheep shorn. Barcaldine became the centre of the strike in February. Thirteen union leaders were sentenced on 20 May 1891 to three years in prison. The strike failed. Many historians think the strike hastened the call for a political party for working people.","src":["slqStrike","nma"]},{"date":"1891 and 1894","title":"Dalgety opens in Queensland","text":"Dalgety and Company opened its first Queensland branch at Rockhampton in 1891, then Brisbane in 1894.","src":["anuDalgety"]},{"date":"August 1891 to June 1892","title":"The Ross River Meatworks","text":"The meatworks at Townsville began to be built in August 1891, and by June 1892 the first cattle were being processed. A blog says it was a solution to an oversupply of cattle in the region, and that it employed 700 workers at its peak. The first shipment of frozen beef, 600 tons, left Townsville on the Otarama for London.","flag":"Other websites disagree with the blog about the condition of the first shipment when it arrived. This resource does not say.","src":["ross"]},{"date":"April 1892","title":"London loan money meets a bank’s withdrawals","text":"The RBA says the proceeds of Queensland Government loans raised in London were used in April 1892 to meet depositors’ withdrawals from the Queensland National Bank.","src":["rba90"]},{"date":"1892","title":"Australia’s sheep flock at 106 million","text":"In 1892 Australia had 106 million sheep.","src":["nma"]},{"date":"May to July 1893","title":"The 1893 bank failures","text":"The Queensland National Bank, the Bank of North Queensland and the Royal Bank of Queensland suspended payment within three days of each other in mid May 1893. A run by Scottish depositors is named as the reason the Queensland National Bank suspended payment. The New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency Company suspended business in July.","src":["rba90","anuNZLM"]},{"date":"1894","title":"Agricultural Lands Purchase Act","text":"The Act let the government buy land from pastoral lessees and open it for selection for family farms. Many of the estates came from owners who were prepared to sell during the depression of the 1890s.","src":["qsaLand"]},{"date":"1895 to 1903","title":"The Federation Drought","text":"The National Museum of Australia says it was the worst drought in Australia’s history, measured by stock losses. By 1903 the sheep flock had almost halved, to 54 million. The country lost more than 40 per cent of its cattle, nearly three million in Queensland alone.","flag":"The Museum’s figures are for Australia as a whole, except the Queensland cattle number.","src":["nma"]},{"date":"1896 to 1897","title":"The Queensland National Bank is found insolvent","text":"Edward Drury died on 3 February 1896. In 1897 a government-appointed committee found the bank deeply insolvent, and the Government passed a Guarantee Act to protect all its deposits for a year. The RBA says the Government was fully repaid in 1918.","flag":"Merrett says the insolvency came out in 1896 after Drury’s death. The RBA says a committee found it in 1897.","src":["rba90","merrett","adbDrury"]},{"date":"1897 to 1904","title":"Laws on Aboriginal workers","text":"In 1897 Queensland passed the Aboriginals Protection and Restriction of the Sale of Opium Act. Under it, an Aboriginal worker could be employed only under a permit from a protector, and employers had to sign an agreement for no more than 12 months. In 1901 a minimum wage of five shillings was set for most workers under the Act, which the Senate committee says was less than one-eighth of the “white wage”. From 1904 the wages of Aboriginal workers could be paid to protectors.","src":["aph"]},{"date":"1902","title":"Dead stock on the road; a Land Act for lessees","text":"In 1902 local newspapers reported more than 2,000 steers dead along the Goondiwindi to Miles route in Queensland. The Land Act of 1902 was largely a relief Act for pastoral lessees hit by the drought.","src":["nma","qsaLand"]},{"date":"1902 to 1904","title":"Bowen Downs after the drought","text":"The local history says that by 1902, 276,244 sheep had died at Bowen Downs and 134,000 remained, and that Mt Cornish had 2,000 cattle left of 27,417. The Scottish Australian Company sold Mt Cornish in 1904.","src":["muttaburra"]},{"date":"After 1902","title":"Smaller holdings","text":"The National Museum of Australia says that by the end of the drought many huge stations had been taken back or foreclosed by banks and divided into smaller holdings. Smaller properties and flocks became normal, and mixed farming was widely taken up. The Guardian reports that Aboriginal people who remained in the Springsure region were taken to missions or reserves from the early 1900s, under laws said to protect them.","src":["nma","guardian"]},{"date":"1915","title":"The Queensland National Pastoral Company","text":"The company was set up in Queensland to acquire the pastoral stations that the Queensland National Bank held.","src":["eoasQNPC"]}],"places":[{"name":"The Darling Downs","where":"South-east Queensland · Country of the Keinjan, Giabal, Jarowair and Barunggam peoples","image":"map","text":["The first squatters arrived in 1840, and the Downs was officially opened for settlement in 1843. Wallis and Burke count 92 conflict events in the Darling Downs district. The photographs of Yandilla (1884) and Jimbour (about 1887) in this resource show stations on the Downs.","By 1877 the Jondaryan run on the Downs was the largest freehold run in Queensland."],"src":["atf","slqShepherd"]},{"name":"Canning Creek","where":"Darling Downs","text":["On 26 October 1848 three Aboriginal women and a child were killed here. Four people saw it, but all said they could not name those who did it. Rolleston wrote that he could not trace them."],"src":["atf"]},{"name":"Kilcoy station","where":"East of the Darling Downs, not on the Downs","text":["On 1 February 1842 flour mixed with strychnine was given to a large group of Aboriginal people by two shepherds employed by the lessee, Evan MacKenzie. At least sixty people died. According to a history by Lauer that the page cites, two escaped convicts reported that 14 or 15 Aboriginal groups met in the Bunya scrub to discuss a response."],"src":["awKilcoy"]},{"name":"Callandoon and Wandai Gumbal","where":"On the present border, and near the present Condamine","text":["Callandoon was the site of the first Native Police camp in what became Queensland, in May 1849. Squatters built barracks at Wandai Gumbal in 1851, at their own expense."],"src":["atf"]},{"name":"Jimbour and Yandilla","where":"Darling Downs","image":"jimbour","text":["The photograph above shows a bullock team outside the woolsheds at Jimbour Station, about 1887. A photograph of sheep washing at Yandilla Station in 1884 is on the Overview page."],"src":["slqShepherd"]},{"name":"Gracemere","where":"Near Rockhampton · Darumbal Country","image":"gracemere","text":["Gracemere was a sheep station run by the Archer family. Black spear grass overran the country, and the station’s sheep were moved to Tambo between 1872 and 1874. When Robert Stubbs Archer became manager in 1880, there were no sheep left.","Rockhampton is in the area that Darumbal Enterprises describes as Darumbal Country."],"src":["fitzroy","darumbal"]},{"name":"Springsure and Cullin-la-ringo","where":"Central Queensland · Country of the Gayiri, Bidjara and Ghungalu peoples","text":["On 17 October 1861 Gayiri people killed 19 members of Horatio Wills’s party at Cullin-la-ringo. Afterwards settlers and then Native Police killed many Gayiri people. The numbers are not known. The Guardian article includes the views of Gayiri, Bidjara and Ghungalu people and of descendants of the Wills family."],"src":["newcastle","guardian"]},{"name":"Tambo (Minnie Downs)","where":"Central-west Queensland","text":["The Archer family bought country here in 1872 and called it Minnie Downs. The sheep were driven there from Gracemere between 1872 and 1874. The National Museum of Australia says later railways and bores, which reached underground water, made it possible for stations to spread further into the dry interior."],"src":["fitzroy","nma"]},{"name":"Barcaldine","where":"Central Queensland","text":["Barcaldine became the centre of the shearers’ strike in February 1891."],"src":["slqStrike"]},{"name":"Townsville (Ross River Meatworks)","where":"North Queensland coast","text":["The meatworks began to be built in August 1891. By June 1892 the first cattle were being processed, and the first shipment of frozen beef left for London."],"src":["ross"]},{"name":"The long paddock","where":"Travelling stock routes, western Queensland","text":["During the drought, drovers looked for feed along travelling stock routes, known as the “long paddock”. Losses of up to 70 per cent were recorded on the road, especially where watering points could be 100 kilometres apart. In 1902 local newspapers reported more than 2,000 steers dead along the Goondiwindi to Miles route."],"src":["nma"]},{"name":"The Moreton district","where":"South-east Queensland · Kabi Kabi, Gubbi Gubbi and Joondoburri peoples are named by Wallis and Burke","text":["The Moreton Bay Penal Settlement closed in 1839. The region was declared a pastoral district on 5 May 1842, and 18 runs were taken up within six years. Cressbrook, widely credited as the first run in the Brisbane Valley, was taken up on 15 July 1841 by David McConnel, backed by substantial family funding from England."],"src":["atfMoreton","kkpac"]},{"name":"The Burnett and Wide Bay","where":"South-east Queensland · Country of the peoples on the Wide Bay Burnett regional list","text":["In 1843 John Eales established Noomoowoolloo at Tiaro in Wide Bay, and Henry Stuart Russell and William Glover took up Burrandowan in the Burnett. Both districts were declared in 1848, though one table gives 1847 for Wide Bay."],"src":["atfWideBay","rdaWBB"]},{"name":"The Maranoa","where":"South-west Queensland · Country of the Mandandanji and Gunggari peoples","text":["After Thomas Mitchell’s expedition of 1846, Allan MacPherson took up Mount Abundance. He left in 1849 because of the levels of frontier conflict. The district was declared open in late 1848, and Surat was established by mid-1850."],"src":["atfMaranoa","maranoaProfile","gunggari"]},{"name":"The Leichhardt district","where":"Central Queensland · Gayiri, Bidjara and Ghungalu Country around Springsure; Gurang Gurang, Wakka Wakka and Wulli Wulli Country at Cracow","text":["Squatters took up Taroom and Juandah in the late 1840s, well before the district was declared open on 10 January 1854."],"src":["atfLeichhardt","guardian"]},{"name":"The Port Curtis district","where":"Central Queensland coast · Bailai, Gurang, Gooreng Gooreng and Taribelang peoples are named for the coast, and Darumbal Country around Rockhampton","text":["The district drew run hunters from 1853, and was proclaimed on 10 January 1854. Gladstone was the first permanent settlement, in 1854, and Rockhampton followed in 1856."],"src":["atfPortCurtis","darumbal"]},{"name":"The Kennedy district","where":"North Queensland · Gugu Badhun Country in the upper Burdekin, among others","text":["The district opened at the end of 1859. Many first runs on the Burdekin date from 1861. Inkerman was taken over by Robert Towns by mortgage in 1864."],"src":["atfKennedy","gugubadhun","nqdry"]},{"name":"The Warrego","where":"South-west Queensland · Country of the Bidjara, Gwamu/Kooma, Gunggari, Kunja, Mandandanji, Mardigan and Murrawarri nations","text":["Joseph Fleming took up more than 100,000 hectares in August 1861 and was bankrupt by November 1862. The district was gazetted in 1864, and interest became apparent in the 1870s when parties from Melbourne began investing."],"src":["atfWarrego","mdba"]},{"name":"Bowen Downs and the Mitchell district","where":"Central-west Queensland · Iningai Country","image":"bowenSheds","text":["Bowen Downs was taken up by William Landsborough and Nat Buchanan with finance from the Scottish Australian Investment Company. Its first cattle arrived in October 1862. By 1902, 276,244 sheep had died there in the drought."],"src":["atfMitchell","jcuBowen","muttaburra"]},{"name":"Burketown and the Burke district","where":"Gulf of Carpentaria · Gangalidda and Garawa Country at Burketown","text":["MacDonald set up Floraville in 1864, and the Landsborough River Company had stocked Beamesbrook by 1865. The district was gazetted in 1865."],"src":["atfBurke","qldGulf"]},{"name":"The Gregory districts and the Channel Country","where":"Far western Queensland · Mithaka and Wangkangurru/Yarluyandi peoples are named by the Western Rivers Alliance","text":["The first runs date from the mid-1860s. Gregory North and Gregory South were gazetted in 1873, and all the available land in Gregory South had been claimed by 1885."],"src":["atfGregoryN","atfGregoryS","wra"]},{"name":"The first banks in Brisbane","where":"Brisbane","image":"qnb","text":["The Bank of New South Wales opened the first bank in Brisbane in November 1850, in Queen Street. The photograph shows the Queensland National Bank in Brisbane in 1891, two years before it suspended payment."],"src":["slqBNSW","rba90"]}],"people":[{"kind":"person","name":"George Leslie","dates":"Darling Downs, 1841","text":"One of the Leslie brothers who took up runs on the Darling Downs from March 1840. His letter of November 1841 is quoted by Wallis and Burke.","src":["atf"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Christopher Rolleston","dates":"Commissioner of Crown Lands from 1843","text":"Reported that at least 13 shepherds had been killed from January 1842 to November 1843. He tried to befriend local Aboriginal people by giving blankets, tomahawks and clothes, and by encouraging settlers to employ them. He wrote that he could not trace those who killed the people at Canning Creek in 1848.","src":["atf"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Frederick Walker","dates":"Commandant, 1849","text":"Set up the first Native Police camp in what became Queensland, at Callandoon, in May 1849.","src":["atf"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Evan MacKenzie","dates":"Lessee of Kilcoy station, 1842","text":"The lessee of Kilcoy station. The record says that two shepherds he employed gave flour mixed with strychnine to a large group of Aboriginal people on 1 February 1842.","src":["awKilcoy"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Horatio Wills","dates":"Cullin-la-ringo, 1861","text":"The leader of the party of which 19 members were killed at Cullin-la-ringo in October 1861.","src":["newcastle","guardian"]},{"kind":"person","name":"The Archer family of Gracemere","text":"Archibald Archer, a member of the family, read a paper on Gracemere in 1972. James Archer was manager in 1871. Robert Stubbs Archer became manager in 1880.","src":["fitzroy"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Edward Wienholt and William Kent’s trustees","dates":"Jondaryan, 1877","text":"The owners of the Jondaryan run, the largest freehold run in Queensland in 1877.","src":["atf"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Dr Ros Kidd","text":"Told the Senate committee that between 4,000 and 5,500 pastoral workers in Queensland each year, from the 1920s to the 1960s, had their labour controlled by the government. She said this was a “guesstimate” and was probably much too low.","src":["aph"]},{"kind":"group","name":"The Keinjan, Giabal, Jarowair and Barunggam peoples","text":"The peoples of the Darling Downs for thousands of years. They practised a burning season to manage the grasses of the plains.","src":["slqShepherd","atf"]},{"kind":"group","name":"The Gayiri, Bidjara and Ghungalu peoples","text":"The Guardian article includes the views of Gayiri, Bidjara and Ghungalu people about Cullin-la-ringo and what followed.","src":["guardian"]},{"kind":"group","name":"The Darumbal people","text":"Traditional Owners of the Rockhampton and Capricorn Coast area, where Gracemere is.","src":["darumbal"]},{"kind":"group","name":"Squatters","text":"Many of the first squatters were younger sons of English and Scottish gentry. They came to sell good raw wool to English mills.","src":["slqShepherd"]},{"kind":"group","name":"Shepherds, hut keepers and stockmen","text":"The squatters’ labour force. In 1846 the Moreton Bay Courier reported wages of £25 a year for good shepherds and £22 for hut keepers, with good rations. Shepherds were among the 13 killed from 1842 to 1843.","src":["slqShepherd","tales","atf"]},{"kind":"group","name":"Selectors","text":"People who took up blocks of land under the Land Acts. Under the Act of 1868 selectors paid annual rent and could freehold agricultural land after three years if they met the conditions.","src":["qsaLand"]},{"kind":"group","name":"Shearers and union leaders","text":"Shearers went on strike in 1891 after pastoralists proposed to cut their wages. Thirteen union leaders were sentenced to three years in prison on 20 May 1891.","src":["slqStrike"]},{"kind":"group","name":"Drovers","text":"Moved stock along travelling stock routes, the “long paddock”, during the drought.","src":["nma"]},{"kind":"group","name":"The Native Police","text":"Troopers recruited in other parts of southern New South Wales and northern Victoria served in the first camp at Callandoon in 1849.","src":["atf"]},{"kind":"org","name":"The Queensland parliament","text":"Passed the Land Acts of 1868, 1876, 1884, 1894 and 1902, and the Aboriginals Protection and Restriction of the Sale of Opium Act of 1897.","src":["qsaLand","aph"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Frederick Gonnerman Dalgety","dates":"1817 to 1894","text":"A merchant whose London-linked business gave wool-growers finance and other services, according to the Australian Dictionary of Biography. His company opened its first Queensland branch in 1891, three years before he died.","src":["adbDalgety","anuDalgety"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Edward Robert Drury","dates":"General manager, Queensland National Bank, 1872 to 1896","text":"The first general manager of the Queensland National Bank. The Australian Dictionary of Biography says he lent £328,000 to Thomas McIlwraith and £67,000 to himself, and that he died on 3 February 1896.","src":["adbDrury","wikiQNB"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Thomas McIlwraith","text":"A Queensland pastoralist, financier and Premier. The RBA says he was on the board of the Queensland National Bank for five years before he became Premier in 1879. Woodley says he was Treasurer in 1891 and a member of the Pastoral Employers’ Association.","src":["rba90","woodley"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Robert Morehead and Boyd Morehead","text":"Robert Morehead managed the Scottish Australian Investment Company in Australia. Wallis and Burke say his son Boyd managed Bowen Downs, took over the company’s runs in the Burke district, and was Premier of Queensland from 20 November 1888 to 12 August 1890.","src":["atfMitchell","adbMorehead"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Nat Buchanan and William Landsborough","text":"The explorers who found the country at Bowen Downs. The Scottish-Australian Company provided the capital to stock it.","src":["jcuBowen","muttaburra"]},{"kind":"person","name":"George Fairbairn (the younger)","dates":"Managing director of the United Mortgage and Agency Company by 1890","text":"Woodley says he managed several Queensland stations and became general manager of the family’s businesses. He called a meeting of Queensland delegates before the pastoralists’ conference in Melbourne in late 1890.","src":["woodley"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Francis Reid (Frank) Murphy","text":"Woodley says he owned extensive Queensland sheep stations, represented Barcoo in the Queensland parliament from 1885 and became president of the Queensland Pastoral Employers’ Association.","src":["woodley"]},{"kind":"person","name":"John Douglas","text":"Later Premier of Queensland. He bought Tooloombah in 1860 with a £5,000 mortgage, was forced to transfer it to the lender in 1867 and was insolvent in 1872.","src":["hodesDouglas"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Richard Goldsbrough","text":"Founded a wool-broking firm in Melbourne in 1848. It acquired Mort and Company of Sydney in 1888 to form Goldsbrough Mort and Company.","src":["anuGM"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Arthur Macalister","text":"Queensland Premier from February 1866. His government said it would issue unsecured government notes after the Queensland loan arrangements failed, and his ministry resigned on 18 July 1866.","src":["hodes1866"]},{"kind":"group","name":"Pastoral and finance companies, and owners who lived elsewhere","text":"Woodley writes that by 1890 Queensland’s biggest properties were generally owned by pastoral and finance companies and that their owners often lived elsewhere, with many in Melbourne.","src":["woodley"]},{"kind":"group","name":"British depositors and investors","text":"Merrett says British deposits were about 40 per cent of the total in Australian banks in 1893. He quotes an estimate that British investors put £18 million into pastoral company shares and £28 million into their debentures. These figures are for all of Australia.","src":["merrett"]},{"kind":"group","name":"Aboriginal pastoral workers and shearers","text":"Woodley says Traditional Owners kept providing labour to the pastoral industry under exploitative conditions, and that many Aboriginal shearers were union members who took part in the 1891 strike.","src":["woodley"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Scottish Australian Investment Company","text":"Set up in Aberdeen in 1840 and incorporated in England in 1856. It financed Bowen Downs, which received its first cattle in October 1862.","src":["anuSAIC","jcuBowen","atfMitchell"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Union Bank of Australia","text":"Founded in London in 1837. It opened in Brisbane in 1858 and was asked to place the Government’s loan in 1864.","src":["unionWiki","hodes1866"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Bank of New South Wales","text":"Based in Sydney. It opened the first Brisbane bank in November 1850.","src":["slqBNSW"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Bank of Australasia","text":"Opened at Ipswich in 1853.","src":["qutBoA","boq"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Bank of Queensland (1863–1866)","text":"Set up in London and opened in Brisbane in August 1863. It suspended payment in the 1866 crisis.","src":["boq"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Agra and Masterman’s Bank, and Overend, Gurney and Company","text":"A London-based bank and a London discount house. Overend Gurney closed on 10 May 1866. Agra and Masterman’s was involved in the failure. Its Sydney agents were to place a Queensland Government loan.","src":["agraWiki","hodes1866"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Queensland National Bank","text":"Set up in Brisbane in 1872. It held the Government’s account from 1879, suspended payment in May 1893 and had its deposits guaranteed by the Government in 1897.","src":["qldHeritageQNB","rba90","adbDrury"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Dalgety and Company","text":"Registered in London in 1884. First Queensland branch at Rockhampton in 1891.","src":["anuDalgety"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Goldsbrough Mort and Company","text":"Formed in 1888. Its Rockhampton manager asked for more advances in June 1890.","src":["anuGM"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Australian Mercantile Land and Finance Company","text":"Registered in England in 1863. It left Queensland in 1865 and held six Queensland stations as loan security by 1904.","src":["anuAMLF","qhAMLF"]},{"kind":"org","name":"New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency Company","text":"Registered in England in 1865. Opened in Brisbane and Rockhampton in 1884. Suspended business in July 1893.","src":["anuNZLM"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Queensland Pastoral Employers’ Association and Pastoralists’ Union of Australia","text":"Employers’ groups formed in April 1889 and in late 1890. They committed to “freedom of contract” in the 1891 strike.","src":["woodley"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Queensland National Pastoral Company","text":"Set up in 1915 to acquire the stations held by the Queensland National Bank.","src":["eoasQNPC"]}],"lives":[{"name":"George Leslie","dates":"Darling Downs, November 1841","text":["George Leslie was one of the Leslie brothers, the first squatters on the Darling Downs. In November 1841 he wrote to his parents about the loss of sheep. He wrote that Aboriginal people had killed or carried away about 60 young ewes. Wallis and Burke read his words as a major reprisal."],"quote":{"lines":["given them what they will not forget in a hurry"],"by":"George Leslie, letter to his parents, November 1841, as quoted by Wallis and Burke."},"after":["Wallis and Burke also note that the violence was costly for squatters, in lives, stock and wages."],"src":["atf"]},{"name":"Christopher Rolleston","dates":"Commissioner of Crown Lands, from 1843","text":["Rolleston reported that at least 13 shepherds had been killed from January 1842 to November 1843, and that thousands of sheep had been killed or driven off. He also tried to befriend local Aboriginal people by giving blankets, tomahawks and clothes. Wallis and Burke, citing the historian M. Shield, say this was not often successful because Aboriginal people did not trust Europeans after violence by shepherds and stockmen against Aboriginal women.","After three Aboriginal women and a child were killed at Canning Creek on 26 October 1848, four people saw it but all said they could not name those who did it. Rolleston wrote that he could not trace them."],"src":["atf"]},{"name":"A hired shepherd, January 1847","dates":"Moreton Bay, 1847","text":["The name of this man is not given in the source read here. In January 1847 the Moreton Bay Courier reported that a man who had been hired as a shepherd and refused to go to his station was sentenced to three months’ hard labour. In 1846 the same paper had complained that some men took the fare and then stayed in Brisbane."],"flag":"These reports are read as quoted in a blog, not on Trove.","src":["tales"]},{"name":"James Archer and Gracemere","dates":"1871","text":["James Archer was the manager of Gracemere, the Archer family’s sheep station near Rockhampton. In 1871 he wrote that the paddocks, after all the expense they had been, were “very little good”. Wool was then nearly two shillings a pound. In 1872 the family bought country at Tambo, and the sheep were driven there between 1872 and 1874."],"quote":{"lines":["very little good"],"by":"James Archer, 1871, as quoted by Archibald Archer in 1972."},"src":["fitzroy"]},{"name":"The thirteen union leaders of 1891","dates":"Barcaldine, 1891","text":["In 1891 shearers refused to work after pastoralists proposed to cut their wages, which were then £1 for every hundred sheep shorn. Barcaldine became the centre of the strike in February. Thirteen union leaders were sentenced on 20 May 1891 to three years in prison. The strike failed. The names of the leaders are not given in the source read here."],"image":"shearers","src":["slqStrike","nma"]}],"know":[{"t":"The Downs were managed for thousands of years with an annual cultural burning season.","src":["slqShepherd","atf"]},{"t":"Wallis and Burke count 92 conflict events on the Darling Downs. About 90 per cent of the conflict took place on pastoral runs.","src":["atf"]},{"t":"Kilcoy is east of the Darling Downs, not on the Downs.","src":["awKilcoy"]},{"t":"The first Native Police troopers in Queensland were recruited from the Murrumbidgee and Murray River areas of southern New South Wales and northern Victoria.","src":["atf"]},{"t":"The word “dummying” meant selecting land on behalf of someone else, against the law. The Act of 1876 tried to stop it, but the State Archives says the efforts were unsuccessful.","src":["qsaLand"]},{"t":"Black spear grass overran the country at Gracemere after the wet years of the 1850s, according to an old sheep farmer quoted by Archibald Archer. It spoiled the wool.","src":["fitzroy"]},{"t":"In 1892 Australia had 106 million sheep. By 1903 the flock had almost halved, to 54 million.","src":["nma"]},{"t":"Drovers looked for feed along the “long paddock”. Losses of up to 70 per cent were recorded on the road.","src":["nma"]},{"t":"The Senate committee says the 1901 minimum wage for Aboriginal workers under the 1897 Act was less than one-eighth of the “white wage”.","src":["aph"]},{"t":"The State Library of Queensland says the squatters’ way of life on the Darling Downs lasted less than fifty years.","src":["slqShepherd"]},{"t":"In 1902 local newspapers reported more than 2,000 steers dead along the Goondiwindi to Miles route.","src":["nma"]},{"t":"In 1880 bank advances in Queensland were 35.6 per cent of the colony’s output. In 1892 they were 91.6 per cent.","src":["merrett"]},{"t":"Dalgety, one of the best-known names in wool, did not open a Queensland branch until 1891.","src":["anuDalgety"]},{"t":"The Bank of New South Wales opened the first bank in Brisbane in November 1850. It was based in Sydney, not London.","src":["slqBNSW"]},{"t":"About 40 per cent of the deposits in Australian banks in 1893 were British, and much of that money matured at the end of the Scottish Whitsun term in mid May.","src":["merrett"]},{"t":"The Queensland Government planned to issue its own notes in 1866 and again in 1893.","src":["hodes1866","rba90"]},{"t":"Wool liens commonly carried interest of 12 per cent a year, including the agent’s commission, according to a Government review of 1909.","src":["ofhc"]},{"t":"The Fairbairn family is estimated to have owned an interest in about one in five Queensland sheep around 1890.","src":["woodley"]},{"t":"The Scottish Australian Company financed Bowen Downs from the 1860s, but four other stations in its archive came to it only in the 1950s.","src":["jcuBowen","jcuScot"]}],"disagree":["The numbers of Aboriginal people killed at Cullin-la-ringo and afterwards are not known. The sources give figures from at least 30 to about 300. The figures are given with their sources.","The 1846 and 1847 newspaper reports on wages and the shepherd sentenced for refusing to go to his station were not read directly. They are read as quoted by a blog.","Sources disagree about the condition of the first shipment of frozen beef from Townsville in 1892, so this resource does not say.","Kilcoy is east of the Darling Downs, not on the Downs. It is included because of its connection to the region.","The National Museum of Australia’s figures for sheep and cattle are for Australia as a whole. Only the figure “nearly three million cattle” is for Queensland alone.","The word “dummying” is explained from general knowledge. The Archives page read does not define it. Please check the meaning.","No source was found for what shepherds were paid in rations, or for the Aboriginal people who lived near Gracemere apart from the Darumbal Enterprises page about Rockhampton.","The Queensland Historical Atlas and Queensland Heritage Register were not used for any fact, because the sites would not load.","The sources quote old letters that contain insulting words about Aboriginal people. Those words are not copied here.","No personal account by an Aboriginal person of this period was found in the sources read here, so this resource cannot give one.","The Folklore and legends page contains stories that are not proven. Each is labelled “Folklore: unproven” and says what is documented and what is doubtful. These stories are not used as facts anywhere else in this resource.","Money from London: Agra and Masterman’s Bank is said to have suspended payment on 6 June 1866 (Wikipedia) and to have been “involved in the failure” of Overend Gurney (Hodes). Hodes also says it telegraphed on 22 July 1866 that it could carry out its agreement.","Money from London: Wikipedia’s page on the Bank of Queensland puts the collapse of Overend Gurney in July 1866. Its page on Agra and Masterman’s says 10 May 1866. This resource uses 10 May for London and 10 July for the news reaching Brisbane.","Money from London: the Wikipedia page on the Bank of Queensland says the four banks in Queensland by late 1862 were “all from other Australian colonies”. The RBA calls the Bank of Australasia and the Union Bank English-based banks. This resource gives each bank’s own origin.","Money from London: Merrett says the Queensland National Bank’s insolvency came out in 1896 after Drury’s death. The RBA says a government committee found it deeply insolvent in 1897.","Money from London: sources differ on who was first manager of Bowen Downs (Nat Buchanan, in the James Cook University archive and Wikipedia, or Alexander “Long” Gordon, in Wallis and Burke) and on its early size and stock (3,000 cattle and 1,500 square miles in Wikipedia, 5,000 cattle in October 1862 and 3,683 square miles eventually in the Muttaburra local history).","Money from London: the Muttaburra local history says the Labour Government raised pastoral rents by as much as 100 per cent in 1902. The Queensland State Archives account used elsewhere in this resource calls the Act of 1902 a Land Act for lessees. The rent claim is not used elsewhere.","Money from London: sources differ on how many strike leaders were jailed in 1891. The State Archives blog says thirteen arrested in March and also 21 men boarding the boat on 26 May. Woodley says fourteen tried and twelve convicted in May. The Timeline in the main resource says thirteen sentenced on 20 May.","Money from London: the Wikipedia page on Bowen Downs says blackbirded South Sea Islanders did much of the labour there in the 1870s and 1880s, and cites one 1876 newspaper item. The newspaper was not read, so this resource does not state it as a fact.","Money from London: the figures for British investment (the £18 million, £28 million and £7 million) are for all of Australia, and come from a journal of the time quoted by Merrett. No Queensland-only figure was found.","Money from London: pages from the Australian Dictionary of Biography, the Queensland Historical Atlas and several archive catalogues could not be fetched in full. Where that happened the source note says the page was read as a search-tool excerpt. Those facts need a check against the full page."],"care":["Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander readers are advised that this resource mentions places where people died. It says that violence against Aboriginal people and violence against settlers happened.","The old records often use insulting words about Aboriginal people. Some of the web pages named in the sources quote them. They are not copied here. This resource uses the words “Aboriginal people” and “Native Police”. The Native Police were Aboriginal men, usually from other parts of Australia, who served under European officers and were used to break up Aboriginal camps.","The author wants to ask Traditional Owners and Aboriginal community members to review this resource before it is used widely. The people to ask include the Giabal, Jarowair, Keinjan and Barunggam peoples for the Darling Downs, the Gayiri, Bidjara and Ghungalu peoples for the Springsure area, and the Darumbal people for Rockhampton. Until that review is done, please treat this resource as a draft."],"readMore":["qsaSearch","trove"],"images":{"yandilla":{"file":"images/sheep-washing-yandilla-1884.jpg","w":960,"h":749,"alt":"A black and white photograph of a creek with a large flock of sheep on the bank and men working on a wooden platform and in yards.","caption":"Sheep washing on Yandilla Station, Darling Downs, 1884. Men work in the creek and in the yards.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_2_295119_Sheep_washing_on_Yandilla_Station,_Darling_Downs,_1884.jpg"},"map":{"file":"images/squatting-map-darling-downs-1864.jpg","w":960,"h":828,"alt":"An old printed map of the Darling Downs with the boundaries of runs, head stations, townships and roads marked in outline.","caption":"Squatting Map of the Darling Downs District, 1864, by J. W. Buxton. It shows the approximate boundaries of runs and their head stations.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_2_180739_Squatting_map_of_the_Darling_Downs_district,_1864.jpg"},"gracemere":{"file":"images/gracemere-station-sketch-1869.jpg","w":960,"h":643,"alt":"A brown-toned sketch of a lake or river with a small sailing boat, and trees and station buildings on the far bank.","caption":"A sketch of Gracemere Station near Rockhampton, about 1869.","credit":"Unknown artist, John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_241830_Sketch_of_Gracemere_Station_near_Rockhampton.jpg"},"jimbour":{"file":"images/bullock-team-jimbour-1887.jpg","w":960,"h":702,"alt":"A black and white photograph of a team of bullocks in front of large wooden woolsheds.","caption":"A bullock team outside the woolsheds at Jimbour Station, Darling Downs, about 1887.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_43351_Bullock_team_outside_the_woolsheds_at_Jimbour_Station,_Queensland,_ca._1887.jpg"},"shearers":{"file":"images/shearers-1880s.jpg","w":960,"h":610,"alt":"A black and white group photograph of many men and boys outside a wooden shearing shed. Some in the front row sit with fleeces.","caption":"Shearers outside a shearing shed, between 1880 and 1890. Four men at the front are shearing with hand shears. The sources read here do not say who the men are.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_2_173975_Portrait_of_shearers_outside_a_shearing_shed,_1880-1890.jpg"},"qnb":{"file":"images/qnb-brisbane-1891.jpg","w":960,"h":702,"alt":"A black and white photograph of a large stone bank building with tall columns along its front and side, seen from a street corner.","caption":"The Queensland National Bank, Brisbane, 1891. Photograph by Poul C. Poulsen.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_114324_Queensland_National_Bank,_Brisbane,_1891.jpg"},"bowenSheds":{"file":"images/bowen-downs-scouring-sheds-1877.jpg","w":960,"h":647,"alt":"A black and white photograph of a brick shed with a tall chimney behind it, trees, fences and two men standing in an open paddock.","caption":"Scouring sheds at Bowen Downs Station, about 1877. Two men stand in the paddock. The sources read here do not say who they were.","credit":"Reckitt and Mills, John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","people":true,"summary":"two men stand near the scouring sheds","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_122270_Scouring_sheds,_Bowen_Downs_Station,_ca._1877.jpg"}},"teacherNotes":["Before you use this in class, please seek advice from Traditional Owners of your area. This resource has not yet been reviewed by Aboriginal community members.","Give students the content warning before they start. Tell them they may stop at any time. Check whether any students have family connections to the events.","Suggested use: students read the Overview and Timeline, then choose one theme (Aboriginal people and the Native Police, land laws, sheep to cattle, or drought) and use the Places and Real lives pages. Then answer two or three discussion questions.","The answers are suggestions. They are behind “Show” buttons. The student version has no answers in its code.","Some of the linked source pages quote offensive words from the records. Look at them before you show them to a class. Preview the pages on Kilcoy, Canning Creek and Cullin-la-ringo before using them.","Money from London: much of the evidence is from histories of all of Australia. Ask students to check each time whether a figure is for Queensland.","Money from London: some pages in the source list could only be read as search-tool excerpts, and these are marked. Look at the full pages before you rely on those facts in class.","Money from London: the Bowen Downs entry includes a documented mass killing in 1866. Read it first and decide how to introduce it. The sources disagree about who managed the station then.","The map of settlement uses “first run” dates and “gazetted” dates, which are different things. Ask students to look at both."],"discuss":[{"q":"Why did the first squatters come to the Darling Downs, and who worked for them?","a":["The State Library of Queensland says they came to sell good raw wool to English mills, and that many were younger sons of English and Scottish gentry. They ruled over a labour force of travelling bush workers, stockmen and shepherds. Labour was recruited in Sydney, with a free fare and a bonus."]},{"q":"How did Aboriginal people use the grasslands before the squatters came? What does the phrase “cultural burning” mean?","a":["The Keinjan, Giabal, Jarowair and Barunggam peoples had lived on the Downs for thousands of years. They practised a burning season to manage the grasses of the plains. Wallis and Burke write that the grasslands were vital to Aboriginal people and were managed by annual cultural burning."]},{"q":"Wallis and Burke describe 1840 to the end of the 1850s on the Downs as almost continuous conflict. Use two figures from the Timeline to show what they mean. Whose deaths are counted, and whose are not recorded?","a":["Examples: 92 conflict events; about 90 per cent on pastoral runs; 16 events and 27 deaths in 1848 (20 Aboriginal people and 7 Europeans); at least 13 shepherds killed from 1842 to 1843.","Students should notice that deaths of Aboriginal people at events such as Canning Creek and Kilcoy were recorded by others and that the people responsible were not named. The numbers for some events are not known."]},{"q":"George Leslie wrote that he had “given them what they will not forget in a hurry”. What does Wallis and Burke’s reading of this tell us? What does a letter like this show, and what does it not show?","a":["They read it as a major reprisal. It shows how a squatter described his response to the loss of sheep. It does not show what Aboriginal people thought, or what happened to them. It is one person’s account."]},{"q":"Look at the land laws in the Timeline (1868, 1876, 1884). What did each try to do? Why do the State Archives and the National Museum of Australia say they did not fully work?","a":["The 1868 Act opened land to selection but let pastoralists make pre-emptive selections along creek and river banks. The 1876 Act tried to stop dummying, but the efforts were unsuccessful. The 1884 Act abolished pre-emptive purchases.","The Museum says squatters found ways to hold on to productive country."]},{"q":"Why did the Archer family move their sheep from Gracemere to Tambo? What does this show about why stations changed from sheep to cattle?","a":["Black spear grass overran the country and spoiled the wool, and the station’s wool book described more and more bales as “seedy”. By 1871 the manager wrote that the paddocks were “very little good”. The sheep were driven to Tambo from 1872 to 1874, and by 1880 no sheep were left at Gracemere.","Gracemere is one example. Students should say that other stations may have had other reasons."]},{"q":"The Federation Drought (1895 to 1903) was the worst in Australia’s history, measured by stock losses. Use the figures to explain what it did to sheep and cattle, and to the squatters.","a":["Australia’s sheep fell from 106 million in 1892 to 54 million in 1903. The country lost more than 40 per cent of its cattle, nearly three million in Queensland. Banks foreclosed on some stations, and the Museum says the drought ended squatter-dominated pastoralism as large stations were divided into smaller holdings.","Note that the Museum’s figures are for Australia as a whole, except the Queensland cattle number."]},{"q":"What is missing from the records used here? What voices are not heard, and where might a historian look for them?","a":["Almost all the records were written by squatters, officials and settlers. Aboriginal people’s own accounts are missing, and so are most shepherds’ and workers’ own accounts. A historian can use oral history, Traditional Owner knowledge and archaeology."]},{"q":"What would you want to ask Traditional Owners before using this resource with a class? List three questions.","a":["Examples: Who are the right people to speak about this Country? Which parts are wrong, or hurt? Is there a name or a story that should be told by them and not by the author? Is it all right to name people who have died?"]},{"q":"Why did a squatter need to borrow money before he had sold any wool? Use the 1909 Government review and the Burke district page.","a":["A run had to be stocked before the lease could be applied for, so stock, fences and wages were paid for first. One wool clip could be held on a remote station until the next shearing, so a lien on the clip let a lender be paid from it. The review says the interest, with the agent’s commission, was commonly 12 per cent a year.","Students should say that the review was written in 1909 and is favourable to the colony."]},{"q":"Which companies on the “Who was really in Queensland?” list were in Queensland in the 1860s, and which were not? Why does it matter to check this?","a":["In Queensland in the 1860s: the Scottish Australian Investment Company (Bowen Downs, 1862), the Union Bank (from 1858), the Bank of New South Wales (1850), the Bank of Australasia (1853) and the London-formed Bank of Queensland (1863). AML&F opened in 1863 and left in 1865. Dalgety did not open until 1891, and the New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency Company not until 1884.","It matters because a well-known name is not proof of a part in Queensland. A claim in an essay needs a source for the place and the date."]},{"q":"Describe the 1866 crisis in your own words. Why did a failure in London stop public works in Queensland?","a":["The Queensland Government depended on borrowing through banks and London agents. A London bank that was to place part of its loan was involved in the failure of Overend Gurney, and the Union Bank cancelled a promised advance and refused government cheques. The Government issued Treasury bills. Works stopped, there were 156 bankruptcies and immigration stopped.","Ask what the sources say about squatters. One squatter wrote that the panic hurt those who were largely in debt or whose credit was not good."]},{"q":"John Douglas lost Tooloombah to the lender in 1867. What does his case show, and what can one case not show?","a":["It shows how a mortgage could end with the lender taking the station, and that the sale price could be less than the debt.","One case cannot show how many squatters lost runs. The statement that “ninety-nine out of every hundred squatters” were insolvent was a member of parliament’s claim in a debate, not a count."]},{"q":"The 1909 Government review says that from 1888 to 1893 values were held up as far as possible by the mortgagees. Why might lenders do this? What could go wrong?","a":["The review says they believed the stagnation must be temporary. Lenders who admitted property was worth less would have had to accept that their loans were worth less.","If values stayed too high, bad loans were not cleared and the failures came later, in 1893. The sources do not give lenders’ own reasons, so students should say their answer is a suggestion."]},{"q":"What does the evidence show about the link between the money and the 1891 shearers’ strike? What does it not show?","a":["It shows that the employers’ leaders and the finance company managers overlapped, and that wages were cut as the wool price fell.","It does not show that a bank or London company ordered the cuts. No source read says so. Students should keep “link” and “cause” separate."]},{"q":"The land taken from Traditional Owners became security for loans. What do the sources say about what the lenders knew? What is missing?","a":["They do not say. The sources say the land was taken with extensive violence, that a mass killing of 13 Aboriginal people was linked to Bowen Downs in 1866, and that Aboriginal people kept working on stations under exploitative conditions. No source read gives a lender’s own words on this.","Students might suggest where to look, such as company letters in the Noel Butlin Archives, or Traditional Owners’ own accounts. Read the Bowen Downs entry yourself first."]},{"q":"Choose two districts on the map. For each, give the year of the first run and the gazettal date. Why are they different, and which tells you more about when settlement began?","a":["Examples: the Darling Downs (first runs 1840, district formally opened 1843) and Port Curtis (first runs 1853, proclaimed 10 January 1854). A gazettal is the Government’s announcement. Runs were often taken up before it, as in the southern Leichhardt.","The first run date tells more about when people arrived. The gazettal date tells more about when the Government began to manage the district. Sources also disagree on some dates, and the map says so."]}],"sources":{"abs150":{"t":"Australian Bureau of Statistics. “150 Years of Queensland’s Economic History: Key Dates, Facts and Figures”. Qld Stats, February 2009.","u":"https://www.abs.gov.au/ausstats/abs@.nsf/Previousproducts/1318.3Feature%20Article14Feb%202009?issue=Feb+2009&num=&opendocument=&prodno=1318.3&tabname=Summary&view=","short":"ABS, 150 years of Queensland","note":"Separation on 10 December 1859, the size of the Gympie, Palmer and Charters Towers rushes, and the 1877 laws."},"fitzroy":{"t":"Archibald Archer. “Fitzroy Waters: From Sheep to Cattle and Coal”. Paper read to the Royal Historical Society of Queensland, 24 August 1972. Journal of the Royal Historical Society of Queensland, vol. 9, no. 3.","u":"https://docslib.org/doc/928996/fitzroy-waters-from-sheep-to-cattle-and-coal","short":"Archer, “Fitzroy Waters”","note":"Written by a member of the Archer family, who used family letters. Only the first part of the paper was read, on the DocsLib copy."},"awKilcoy":{"t":"Australian Wars and Resistance (website). “Massacre at Kilcoy Station”, 1 February 1842.","u":"https://www.australianwars.net/event.php?id=379","short":"Australian Wars and Resistance, Kilcoy","note":"Lists the sources for the Kilcoy poisoning, including the Sydney Morning Herald of 5 December 1842."},"darumbal":{"t":"Darumbal Enterprises. Darumbal History.","u":"https://darumbal.com.au/darumbal-culture/darumbal-history/","short":"Darumbal Enterprises"},"nma":{"t":"National Museum of Australia. “Federation Drought”. Defining Moments (website), updated 10 June 2025.","u":"https://www.nma.gov.au/defining-moments/resources/federation-drought","short":"National Museum of Australia, Federation Drought","note":"Sheep and cattle numbers, stock losses on the droving routes, foreclosures by banks, and the break-up of large stations. Many of its figures are for Australia as a whole. Its Queensland figure is for cattle only."},"ross":{"t":"Trisha Fielding. “Ross River Meatworks”. North Queensland History (blog), 26 October 2015.","u":"https://northqueenslandhistory.blogspot.com/2015/10/ross-river-meatworks.html","short":"North Queensland History, Ross River Meatworks","note":"Dates of building and of the first shipment of frozen beef. Other websites disagree with this blog about the condition of the first shipment when it arrived in London, so this app does not state it."},"qsaLand":{"t":"Queensland State Archives. “Part 5: Key Legislation for Land Selections since 1860, summarised”. Stories from the Archives, 3 April 2018.","u":"https://blogs.archives.qld.gov.au/2018/04/03/part-5-key-legislation-for-land-selections-since-1860-summarised/","short":"QSA, land selection laws","note":"A summary of the Land Acts of 1860, 1868, 1876, 1884, 1897, 1902, 1906 and 1910, written from research by the historian Ruth Kerr."},"qsaSearch":{"t":"Queensland State Archives. Archives Search (online catalogue).","u":"https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/","short":"Queensland State Archives, Archives Search","note":"To read more: search for pastoral lease records, Commissioner of Crown Lands letters and Native Police records."},"aph":{"t":"Parliament of Australia, Senate Legal and Constitutional Affairs References Committee. “Unfinished business: Indigenous stolen wages”, Chapter 2, “Life under the protection acts”. 2006.","u":"https://www.aph.gov.au/parliamentary_business/committees/senate/legal_and_constitutional_affairs/completed_inquiries/2004-07/stolen_wages/report/c02","short":"Senate report on stolen wages, chapter 2","note":"Describes the Queensland Act of 1897, the permit and agreement rules for Aboriginal workers, and the control of wages."},"slqStrike":{"t":"Queensland State Archives (published by the John Oxley Library blog). “Shearers’ Strike 1891”. State Library of Queensland blog, 5 June 2012.","u":"https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/blog/shearers-strike-1891","short":"SLQ blog, Shearers’ Strike 1891","note":"The causes, the centre at Barcaldine, the trials and sentences, and the result of the 1891 strike."},"slqShepherd":{"t":"State Library of Queensland. “The ‘Shepherd Kings of the Darling Downs’: essay”. From the exhibition Grass Dukes and Shepherd Kings.","u":"https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/discover/exhibitions/grass-dukes-shepherd-kings/shepherd-kings-darling-downs-essay","short":"SLQ, Shepherd Kings of the Darling Downs","note":"The peoples of the Downs and their burning season, the first squatters, the squatters’ labour force, wool profits, and the decline of the squatters by 1900."},"tales":{"t":"K. C. Sbeghen. “Reluctant Shepherds”. Tales from Colonial Queensland (blog), 4 September 2013.","u":"http://talesfromcolonialqueensland.blogspot.com/2013/09/reluctant-shepherds.html","short":"Tales from Colonial Queensland, “Reluctant Shepherds”","note":"Quotes the Moreton Bay Courier of 15 August 1846, 19 September 1846 and 2 January 1847 about hiring shepherds and hut keepers. A blog is a secondary source. The newspaper pages were not read directly."},"guardian":{"t":"Ella Archibald-Binge. “The massacre of sleeping settlers that unleashed a savage war in central Queensland”. The Guardian Australia, 5 August 2025.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/aug/05/cullin-la-ringo-massacre-central-qld-queensland-tom-wills-family-ntwnfb","short":"The Guardian, Cullin-la-ringo (2025)","note":"A news feature that includes the views of Gayiri, Bidjara and Ghungalu people and of descendants of the Wills family. It has a warning about racist language in historical records."},"trove":{"t":"National Library of Australia. Trove: digitised newspapers.","u":"https://trove.nla.gov.au/","short":"Trove","note":"To read more: search for the Moreton Bay Courier (1846 to 1847), the Rockhampton Bulletin (1861) and the Queenslander (1890s)."},"newcastle":{"t":"Centre for 21st Century Humanities, University of Newcastle. “Colonial Frontier Massacres in Central and Eastern Australia 1788–1930”: record for Cullin la Ringo, 17 October 1861.","u":"https://c21ch.newcastle.edu.au/colonialmassacres/detail.php?r=648","short":"University of Newcastle, Colonial Frontier Massacres, Cullin la Ringo","note":"A research database. Its record cites newspapers of 1861 and the historians Carment, Reid and Perrin."},"atf":{"t":"Lynley A. Wallis and Heather Burke. “Darling Downs Pastoral District”. Archaeology on the Frontier (website).","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/darling-downs/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Darling Downs district","note":"The page quotes old letters that contain insulting words about Aboriginal people. Those words are not copied here."},"wikiReadford":{"t":"“Harry Readford”. Wikipedia.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harry_Readford","short":"Wikipedia, Harry Readford","note":"A summary of the cattle drive, the trial and the link to Captain Starlight. Its list of further reading includes books by Patrick McCarthy and John Deckert."},"bcRoma1873":{"t":"“Roma District Court”. The Brisbane Courier, 18 February 1873, p. 3.","u":"https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/1309393","short":"Brisbane Courier, 18 February 1873","note":"The report of the trial of Henry Redford, the verdict and the judge’s remark. Read through an archived copy of the Trove page."},"qsaScotchman":{"t":"Queensland State Archives. “James McPherson – The Wild Scotchman”. Stories from the Archives (blog), 14 May 2021.","u":"https://blogs.archives.qld.gov.au/2021/05/14/james-mcpherson-the-wild-scotchman/","short":"Queensland State Archives, the Wild Scotchman","note":"The robberies of 1865, the capture, the sentence and the stories of his manners and his treasure."},"wikiMatilda":{"t":"“Waltzing Matilda”. Wikipedia.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waltzing_Matilda","short":"Wikipedia, Waltzing Matilda","note":"The writing of the song, the death of Samuel Hoffmeister and the doubts about the link."},"wikiBlackStump":{"t":"“Black stump”. Wikipedia.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_stump","short":"Wikipedia, Black stump","note":"The meaning of the phrase and the three towns that claim it."},"wikiLeichhardtTree":{"t":"“Leichhardt Tree, Taroom”. Wikipedia. Based on the Queensland Heritage Register listing.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leichhardt_Tree,_Taroom","short":"Wikipedia, Leichhardt Tree, Taroom","note":"The history of the tree and what is and is not known about its mark."},"hodes1866":{"t":"Jeremy Hodes. “The Queensland financial crisis of 1866”. Queensland History (blog), 15 September 2011.","u":"https://queenslandhistory.blogspot.com/2011/09/queensland-financial-crisis-of-1866.html","short":"Hodes, 1866 crisis","note":"A blog by a historian, with footnotes to S. J. Butlin, A. C. V. Melbourne (Daily Mail, 1927) and other sources. Used for the loans, the dates and the results of the 1866 crisis."},"boq":{"t":"“Bank of Queensland (1863–1866)”. Wikipedia.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bank_of_Queensland_(1863%E2%80%931866)","short":"Wikipedia, Bank of Queensland (1863–1866)","note":"A bank set up in London that opened in Brisbane on 13 August 1863. It also lists the four banks already in Queensland by late 1862."},"agraWiki":{"t":"“Agra and Masterman’s Bank”. Wikipedia.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agra_and_Masterman%27s_Bank","short":"Wikipedia, Agra and Masterman’s Bank","note":"Says the bank suspended payment on 6 June 1866 after the panic that began when Overend Gurney closed on 10 May 1866."},"rba90":{"t":"Reserve Bank of Australia. “The 1890s Depression”. In: A History of Last-Resort Lending and Other Support for Troubled Financial Institutions in Australia, Research Discussion Paper 2001-07.","u":"https://www.rba.gov.au/publications/rdp/2001/2001-07/1890s-depression.html","short":"RBA, The 1890s Depression","note":"Section 6.5 covers Queensland: the banks that suspended in May 1893, the Government’s response and the Queensland National Bank."},"merrett":{"t":"David T. Merrett. “The Australian Bank Crashes of the 1890s Revisited”. Centre for Economic History, Australian National University, Discussion Paper 2013-05, April 2013.","u":"https://cbe.anu.edu.au/researchpapers/CEH/WP201305.pdf","short":"Merrett, The Australian Bank Crashes of the 1890s","note":"A research paper on all the colonies. Its figures are for Australia unless the text says Queensland."},"woodley":{"t":"Peter Woodley. “Entangled experiences of class: The 1891 Queensland bush workers’ strike”. Australian Journal of Biography and History, no. 8, 2024. ANU Press.","u":"https://doi.org/10.22459/ajbh.08.2024.07","short":"Woodley, The 1891 Queensland bush workers’ strike","note":"A peer-reviewed article with footnotes to Svensen’s “The Shearers’ War” and to newspapers of 1890 and 1891."},"qsaStrike":{"t":"Queensland State Archives. “Shear power: Shearer’s strike of 1891”. Stories from the Archives, 21 December 2021.","u":"https://blogs.archives.qld.gov.au/2021/12/21/shear-power-shearers-strike-of-1891/","short":"QSA, Shear power","note":"Says employers cut wages and entitlements in late 1890 as the wool price fell. It gives thirteen arrests in March and also says 21 men boarded the boat to St Helena on 26 May."},"ofhc":{"t":"Queensland Government. “Our First Half-Century: A Review of Queensland Progress, based upon Official Information”. Brisbane, Government Printer, 1909 (Project Gutenberg copy).","u":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/39495/pg39495-images.html","short":"Queensland Government, Our First Half-Century (1909)","note":"An official review written in 1909 and favourable to the colony. It describes wool liens and the property values of the 1880s. Treat its opinions as those of 1909."},"hodesDouglas":{"t":"Jeremy Hodes. “squatter” label page, Queensland History (blog). Extract from his thesis on John Douglas, about Tooloombah station.","u":"https://queenslandhistory.blogspot.com/search/label/squatter","short":"Hodes, John Douglas and Tooloombah","note":"Gives the Tooloombah mortgage, the transfer in 1867 and the insolvency in 1872, with footnotes. It also quotes a squatter on the panic of 1866."},"anuDalgety":{"t":"Noel Butlin Archives Centre, Australian National University. “Dalgety and Company Limited Brisbane Branch deposit” (administrative history).","u":"https://archivescollection.anu.edu.au/index.php/dalgety-and-company-limited-brisbane-branch-deposit","short":"ANU Archives, Dalgety and Company","note":"Says Dalgety and Company Limited was registered in London on 29 April 1884 and opened branches in Rockhampton (1891) and Brisbane (1894). The date for Townsville (1896) was read as a search-tool excerpt."},"adbDalgety":{"t":"R. M. Hartwell. “Dalgety, Frederick Gonnerman (1817–1894)”. Australian Dictionary of Biography, volume 4, 1972.","u":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/dalgety-frederick-gonnerman-283","short":"ADB, Frederick Gonnerman Dalgety","note":"About Dalgety’s London-linked business and how it financed wool-growers. Read as an excerpt shown by a search tool, because the site blocks automated fetching. Check it against the full page."},"adbDrury":{"t":"Australian Dictionary of Biography. “Drury, Edward Robert (1832–1896)”.","u":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/drury-edward-robert-3445","short":"ADB, Edward Robert Drury","note":"Says Drury became general manager of the Queensland National Bank in 1872, lent £328,000 to Thomas McIlwraith and £67,000 to himself, and died on 3 February 1896. It mentions the stories that he faked his death and that his coffin held stones, and says they were never proved. Read as an excerpt shown by a search tool, because the site blocks automated fetching. Check it against the full page."},"qldHeritageQNB":{"t":"Queensland Heritage Register. “Queensland National Bank (former)”, Townsville, entry 600905.","u":"https://apps.des.qld.gov.au/heritage-register/detail/?id=600905","short":"Queensland Heritage Register, Queensland National Bank (Townsville)","note":"Says the bank was set up in Brisbane in 1872 by a group of Queensland businessmen and held the government’s account from 1879 to 1921. Read as an excerpt shown by a search tool, because the site blocks automated fetching. Check it against the full page."},"wikiQNB":{"t":"“Queensland National Bank”. Wikipedia.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queensland_National_Bank","short":"Wikipedia, Queensland National Bank","note":"Says the bank was established in Brisbane in 1872, had a London branch by 1914, and that Edward Drury was general manager from 1872 to 1896."},"eoasQNPC":{"t":"Guide to Australian Business Records. “Queensland National Pastoral Company Pty Ltd (1915– )”.","u":"https://www.eoas.info/GABR/biogs/ABE1729b.htm","short":"Guide to Australian Business Records, Queensland National Pastoral Company","note":"Says the company was incorporated in Queensland in 1915 to acquire the stations held by the Queensland National Bank."},"anuAMLF":{"t":"Noel Butlin Archives Centre, Australian National University. “Australian Mercantile Land and Finance Company Limited” (history).","u":"https://archivescollection.anu.edu.au/index.php/australian-mercantile-land-and-finance-company-limited","short":"ANU Archives, Australian Mercantile Land and Finance","note":"Says the company was registered in England on 5 November 1863, opened its first Australian office in Brisbane that year and moved its business to Victoria in 1865. Read as an excerpt shown by a search tool, because the site blocks automated fetching. Check it against the full page."},"qhAMLF":{"t":"Queensland Historical Atlas. “Empire and agribusiness: the Australian Mercantile Land and Finance Company”.","u":"https://www.qhatlas.com.au/empire-and-agribusiness-australian-mercantile-land-and-finance-company","short":"Queensland Historical Atlas, AML&F","note":"Says that by 1904 the company held 80 stations as security for loans, six in Queensland, after taking foreclosed land in the 1890s, and that it bought its first Queensland station, Retro, in 1906. The page timed out when fetched, so it was read as a search-tool excerpt."},"qhPast":{"t":"Queensland Historical Atlas. “Pastoralism 1860s–1915”.","u":"https://www.qhatlas.com.au/pastoralism-1860s-1915","short":"Queensland Historical Atlas, Pastoralism 1860s–1915","note":"Says much of the 1860s expansion came largely from Victorian wealth, that artesian water was tapped after 1880 and that Queensland sheep numbers peaked at 21 million by 1892. The page timed out when fetched, so it was read as a search-tool excerpt."},"anuNZLM":{"t":"Noel Butlin Archives Centre, Australian National University. “New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency Company Rockhampton Branch deposit” (administrative history).","u":"https://archivescollection.anu.edu.au/index.php/new-zealand-loan-and-mercantile-agency-company-rockhampton-branch-deposit","short":"ANU Archives, New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency","note":"Says the company was registered in England in April 1865, opened Brisbane and Rockhampton branches in 1884, suspended business in July 1893 and was reconstructed on 10 May 1894."},"anuGM":{"t":"Noel Butlin Archives Centre, Australian National University. “Goldsbrough Mort and Company Head Office records” and the item “Correspondence from Rockhampton Manager requesting further advances to continue business”, June 1890.","u":"https://archivescollection.anu.edu.au/index.php/correspondence-to-goldsbrough-mort-and-company-requesting-further-advances-to-continue-business","short":"ANU Archives, Goldsbrough Mort and Company","note":"The head office history says the company was formed in 1888 when R. Goldsbrough and Company, a Melbourne wool-broking firm founded in 1848, acquired Mort and Company of Sydney. The June 1890 item is a catalogue entry that was not opened. Read as an excerpt shown by a search tool, because the site blocks automated fetching. Check it against the full page."},"anuSAIC":{"t":"Noel Butlin Archives Centre, Australian National University. “The Scottish Australian Investment Company Limited” (authority record).","u":"https://archivescollection.anu.edu.au/index.php/scottish-australian-investment-company-limited","short":"ANU Archives, Scottish Australian Investment Company","note":"Says the firm was set up in Aberdeen in 1840, began in Australia in 1841 and was incorporated in England on 27 October 1856."},"jcuBowen":{"t":"James Cook University Library Special Collections. “Bowen Downs Station Records”.","u":"https://libserver.jcu.edu.au/specials/Archives/bowen.html","short":"JCU Library, Bowen Downs station records","note":"Says the Scottish-Australian Company provided the capital for Bowen Downs and that Nat Buchanan was the first manager. It lists shearers’ and men’s wage ledgers from 1872 to 1948."},"jcuScot":{"t":"James Cook University Library Special Collections. “Scottish Australian Co. Ltd Archive”.","u":"https://libserver.jcu.edu.au/specials/Archives/scot.html","short":"JCU Library, Scottish Australian Co. archive","note":"Says Mt Emu Plains, Kynuna, Albion Downs and Lucknow became part of the company’s holdings only in the early 1950s."},"muttaburra":{"t":"Muttaburra (community website). “Pastoral History of the Aramac Shire”, adapted from text by Lesley Cowper.","u":"https://www.muttaburra.com/historical-milestones/pastoral-history-of-the-aramac-shire/","short":"Muttaburra, Pastoral history of the Aramac Shire","note":"A local history without footnotes. It gives the Landsborough River Company shares, the stock numbers at Bowen Downs and the losses in the drought to 1902."},"wikiBowen":{"t":"“Bowen Downs Station”. Wikipedia.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bowen_Downs_Station","short":"Wikipedia, Bowen Downs Station","note":"Names Iningai as the Traditional Owners and Nat Buchanan as the first manager. Its claim about blackbirded South Sea Islander labour rests on one item that was not read."},"unionWiki":{"t":"“Union Bank of Australia”. Wikipedia.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union_Bank_of_Australia","short":"Wikipedia, Union Bank of Australia","note":"Says the bank was founded in London in 1837 and opened a Brisbane branch in 1858."},"slqBNSW":{"t":"State Library of Queensland (John Oxley Library blog). “Queensland’s first bank opened its doors 160 years”, 2 June 2010.","u":"https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/blog/queenslands-first-bank-opened-its-doors-160-years","short":"SLQ blog, Queensland’s first bank","note":"Says the Bank of New South Wales opened a branch in Queen Street, Brisbane, in November 1850."},"qutBoA":{"t":"QUT Digital Collections. “Bank of Australasia, Ipswich, Queensland”.","u":"https://digitalcollections.qut.edu.au/6737/","short":"QUT, Bank of Australasia, Ipswich","note":"Gives 7 November 1853 as the opening of the bank’s Ipswich branch. Read as an excerpt shown by a search tool, because the site blocks automated fetching. Check it against the full page."},"adbDeuchar":{"t":"Australian Dictionary of Biography. “Deuchar, John”, life summary.","u":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/lifesummary/deuchar-john-3404","short":"ADB, John Deuchar (life summary)","note":"The excerpt says Deuchar worked for the North British Australasian Company from 1842 to 1855 and managed Goomburra and Rosenthal. Read as an excerpt shown by a search tool, because the site blocks automated fetching. Check it against the full page."},"adbMorehead":{"t":"Australian Dictionary of Biography. “Morehead, Robert Archibald Alison”.","u":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/morehead-robert-archibald-alison-2478","short":"ADB, Robert Archibald Alison Morehead","note":"The excerpt says he managed the Scottish Australian Investment Company in Australia from 1841. Read as an excerpt shown by a search tool, because the site blocks automated fetching. Check it against the full page."},"anuBurnsPhilp":{"t":"Noel Butlin Archives Centre, Australian National University. “Changing Times”, in the exhibition on Burns Philp.","u":"https://archives.anu.edu.au/exhibitions/merchants-pacific-rise-and-fall-burns-philp/changing-times","short":"ANU Archives, Burns Philp, Changing Times","note":"Says two of Burns Philp’s three banks, the Queensland National Bank and the Bank of North Queensland, collapsed in the 1890s. Read as an excerpt shown by a search tool, because the site blocks automated fetching. Check it against the full page."},"atfMitchell":{"t":"Lynley A. Wallis and Heather Burke. “Mitchell Pastoral District”. Archaeology on the Frontier.","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/mitchell/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Mitchell district","note":"The first runs of 1862, the finance for Bowen Downs from the Scottish Australian Investment Company, and a mass killing in 1866 linked to Bowen Downs and Aramac. It names Alexander Gordon, not Nat Buchanan, as first manager."},"atfBurke":{"t":"Lynley A. Wallis and Heather Burke. “Burke Pastoral District”. Archaeology on the Frontier.","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/burke/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Burke district","note":"Says run holders had to stock the land before applying to lease it, and that the first pastoralists were often backed by finance from the big cities. It mentions the Landsborough River Company at Beamesbrook by 1865."},"atfKennedy":{"t":"Lynley A. Wallis and Heather Burke. “Kennedy Pastoral District”. Archaeology on the Frontier.","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/kennedy/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Kennedy district","note":"The first runs of 1861 and the mortgage takeover of Inkerman in 1864."},"atfWarrego":{"t":"Lynley A. Wallis and Heather Burke. “Warrego Pastoral District”. Archaeology on the Frontier.","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/warrego/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Warrego district","note":"The first runs of 1861 to 1863 and the loss of Joseph Fleming’s runs."},"atfIndex":{"t":"Lynley A. Wallis and Heather Burke. “Queensland Pastoral Districts”. Archaeology on the Frontier (website).","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Queensland pastoral districts","note":"A table of the gazettal year and size of each pastoral district. Used for the “gazetted” years on the map."},"atfMoreton":{"t":"Lynley A. Wallis and Heather Burke. “Moreton Pastoral District”. Archaeology on the Frontier.","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/moreton/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Moreton district","note":"The Cressbrook run of July 1841 and the declaration of the district on 5 May 1842. It names the Kabi Kabi, Gubbi Gubbi and Joondoburri peoples in the north of the district."},"atfWideBay":{"t":"Heather Burke and Lynley A. Wallis. “Wide Bay and Burnett Pastoral Districts”. Archaeology on the Frontier.","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/wide-bay/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Wide Bay and Burnett districts","note":"Noomoowoolloo at Tiaro and Burrandowan in 1843. Its text says both districts were declared in 1848, but its table gives 1847 for Wide Bay."},"atfMaranoa":{"t":"Lynley A. Wallis and Heather Burke. “Maranoa Pastoral District”. Archaeology on the Frontier.","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/maranoa/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Maranoa district","note":"The first runs after Mitchell’s 1846 expedition and the opening of the district in late 1848. It includes old accounts that use insulting words about Aboriginal people. Those words are not copied here."},"atfLeichhardt":{"t":"Lynley A. Wallis and Heather Burke. “Leichhardt Pastoral District”. Archaeology on the Frontier.","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/leichhardt/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Leichhardt district","note":"The late 1840s runs on the Dawson and the opening of the district on 10 January 1854. It names the Gurang Gurang, Wakka Wakka and Wulli Wulli people for Cracow."},"atfPortCurtis":{"t":"Heather Burke and Lynley A. Wallis. “Port Curtis Pastoral District”. Archaeology on the Frontier.","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/port-curtis/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Port Curtis district","note":"The Archers at Gracemere in 1853, the proclamation on 10 January 1854, Gladstone in 1854 and Rockhampton in 1856, and the Bailai, Gurang, Gooreng Gooreng and Taribelang people named in an account of 1802."},"atfGregoryN":{"t":"Lynley A. Wallis and Heather Burke. “Gregory North Pastoral District”. Archaeology on the Frontier.","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/gregory-north/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Gregory North district","note":"First runs from the mid-1860s, gazetted in 1873, and a stock estimate for 1861 that conflicts with those dates."},"atfGregoryS":{"t":"Lynley A. Wallis and Heather Burke. “Gregory South Pastoral District”. Archaeology on the Frontier.","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/gregory-south/","short":"Wallis and Burke, Gregory South district","note":"Gives two accounts of the first run, one from 1866 and one from 1868, and says all available land was claimed by 1885."},"rdaWBB":{"t":"Regional Development Australia Wide Bay Burnett. Home page (acknowledgement of Traditional Custodians).","u":"https://www.rdawidebayburnett.org.au/","short":"RDA Wide Bay Burnett","note":"Lists the groups it acknowledges as Traditional Custodians of the whole Wide Bay Burnett region, without saying which group belongs to which district. It is a regional development body, not a Traditional Owner group."},"kkpac":{"t":"Kabi Kabi Peoples Aboriginal Corporation. “Kabi Kabi Country”.","u":"https://www.kkpac.org.au/kabi-kabi-country","short":"Kabi Kabi Peoples Aboriginal Corporation, Kabi Kabi Country","note":"Describes Kabi Kabi Country from the Pine River near Redcliffe to the Isis River near Hervey Bay, including the Mary River."},"maranoaProfile":{"t":"Maranoa Regional Council community profile. “About the profile areas”.","u":"https://profile.id.com.au/maranoa/about","short":"Maranoa Regional Council profile","note":"Names the Mandandanji and Gunggari people as the traditional owners of the council area. Read as an excerpt shown by a search tool, because the site blocks automated fetching. Check it against the full page."},"gunggari":{"t":"Gunggari Native Title Aboriginal Corporation. “Gunggari Country”.","u":"https://www.gunggaripbc.com.au/gunggari-country/","short":"Gunggari Native Title Aboriginal Corporation","note":"Places Gunggari Country between Charleville, Mitchell, St George and Bollon, with Mandandanji Country to the east and Kooma Country to the south. It says the culture is described as “Gunggari”, “Kogai”, “Unggari” or “Gnorree”."},"mdba":{"t":"Murray–Darling Basin Authority. “Warrego catchment”.","u":"https://www.mdba.gov.au/basin/catchments/northern-basin-catchments/warrego-catchment","short":"Murray–Darling Basin Authority, Warrego catchment","note":"Lists the nations whose lands the Warrego catchment takes in or closely borders, and says pastoralists took up land there in the 1860s."},"gugubadhun":{"t":"Gugu Badhun Aboriginal Corporation RNTBC. “Our Country”.","u":"http://www.gugubadhun.com/our-country","short":"Gugu Badhun Aboriginal Corporation, Our Country","note":"Says Gugu Badhun Country centres on the upper Burdekin River catchment, including the Valley of Lagoons. Read as an excerpt shown by a search tool, because the site blocks automated fetching. Check it against the full page."},"nqdry":{"t":"NQ Dry Tropics. “Traditional Owners”.","u":"https://www.nqdrytropics.com.au/project-category/traditional-owners/","short":"NQ Dry Tropics, Traditional Owners","note":"Lists the Traditional Owner groups it acknowledges across the Burdekin Dry Tropics region, without saying which belongs to which pastoral district."},"qldGulf":{"t":"Queensland Government ministerial media statement, 3 November 2022. “Land of the Gulf Country returned to Traditional Owners”.","u":"https://statements.qld.gov.au/statements/96480","short":"Queensland Government statement, Gulf Country land handover","note":"Names the Gangalidda and Garawa people and the land at Burketown."},"wra":{"t":"Western Rivers Alliance. “The Channel Country’s Traditional Owners”.","u":"https://westernrivers.org.au/the-channel-countrys-traditional-owners/","short":"Western Rivers Alliance, Channel Country Traditional Owners","note":"Quotes a Mithaka Traditional Owner and a woman who calls the Channel Country Wangkangurru/Yarluyandi Country."},"naturalEarth":{"short":"Natural Earth, state outline","t":"Natural Earth. “Admin 1 – States, Provinces” (1:10m), Queensland outline.","u":"https://www.naturalearthdata.com/downloads/10m-cultural-vectors/10m-admin-1-states-provinces/","note":"Natural Earth data is in the public domain. The outline was simplified for this map. The map is not a survey and the positions of the districts are approximate."}},"folklore":{"intro":"Many yarns about cattle and sheep country are still told in outback towns. This page collects six of them. Each can be found in a published source. For each one, read the story first. Then read what we actually know.","banner":{"head":"Stories people told, not proven history","text":["Read this first: the stories on this page are folklore. They are not proven history.","These are yarns, legends and tall tales that people have told about this period. Each one really exists in a published source, and the source is linked. But the stories themselves are largely unproven. Some may be partly true. Some are probably wrong.","Each story has a “What we actually know” part. It says what is documented and why the rest is doubtful. Do not use anything on this page as a fact in an essay or a test.","This page does not include Aboriginal Dreaming stories or any sacred or restricted stories."]},"stories":[{"title":"Harry Readford and the 1,000 stolen cattle","where":"Bowen Downs station to South Australia · 1870; trial at Roma · February 1873","text":["The story says that in 1870 a stockman named Harry Readford stole about 1,000 cattle from Bowen Downs station in central Queensland. He drove them about 1,300 kilometres south through the Channel Country to South Australia, and sold them for £5,000.","In the story, a white bull that he traded for rations was later recognised and led the station men to him. He was tried at Roma in 1873. The jury found him not guilty because it was so impressed by what he had done. The author Rolf Boldrewood is said to have based the outlaw Captain Starlight, in the novel Robbery Under Arms, on Readford."],"from":"The main sources are the Wikipedia article on Harry Readford, which lists books about him including Patrick McCarthy’s “The Man Who Was Starlight”, and a report of the Roma trial in The Brisbane Courier of 18 February 1873.","known":["What is documented: The Brisbane Courier of 18 February 1873 reports that Henry Redford was tried at the Roma District Court for stealing cattle from Bowen Downs in March 1870. The jury found him not guilty. The report says Judge Charles Blakeney joined in the surprise at the verdict. It says he had the foreman repeat it and then said, “Thank God, gentlemen, that verdict is yours, not mine.” A drover from Bowen Downs identified the white bull in court.","Why the rest is doubtful: The report does not say why the jury decided as it did, so the claim that it admired the drive is a guess. The charge read out in court named 401 cattle (100 bullocks, 100 cows, 100 heifers, 100 steers and one bull), not 1,000. Wikipedia says Boldrewood described Captain Starlight as a mix of several people, and that Readford was never known by that name. It says a different bushranger, Frank Pearson, had used the name Captain Starlight since 1868."],"src":["wikiReadford","bcRoma1873"]},{"title":"The Wild Scotchman and his buried sovereigns","where":"Gayndah Road and Gin Gin · November 1865 to March 1866","text":["James McPherson, known as the Wild Scotchman, robbed mail coaches on the Gayndah Road in 1865. The story says he was polite to the people he robbed. When he found letters with no money in them, he folded them up and put them back in their envelopes. It says he posted worthless cheques worth £1,700 to the Governor, Sir George Bowen.","One story says he escaped from a ship at Mackay by slipping his hands out of handcuffs that were too big for them, or by using a makeshift key. Another says that after he was released he had about 1,000 sovereigns, and that he put them in a kettle and buried them somewhere near Aramara."],"from":"The Queensland State Archives published these stories in a 2021 blog post, “James McPherson – The Wild Scotchman”. The post uses wording such as “reportedly”, “reports vary” and “it’s said”.","known":["What is documented: McPherson robbed six mail coaches and four people on the Gayndah Road on 27 November 1865. Locals from a station near Gin Gin captured him in March 1866. He was tried at Maryborough and sentenced to 25 years. He was released on 22 December 1874 after a petition. The State Archives holds the index of prisoners admitted to St Helena Island, which lists him and his false names.","Why the rest is doubtful: The post says that reports vary on how he escaped at Mackay. It says the treasure is “said” to be buried and that stories of it “persisted”. It does not say the kettle was ever found. It does not name a source for the folded letters or the cheques. These stories are unproven."],"src":["qsaScotchman"]},{"title":"Waltzing Matilda and the swagman who died at a billabong","where":"Dagworth and Kynuna, Winton district · 1894 and 1895","text":["A popular story says the song “Waltzing Matilda” was based on a real man, a striking shearer named Samuel Hoffmeister. Hoffmeister died at a billabong near Kynuna in September 1894. The story says the poet Banjo Paterson visited that billabong in 1895, heard about the death and wrote the song about it.","Some tellings add that the song is a secret song about the shearers’ strike."],"from":"The Wikipedia article “Waltzing Matilda” covers the folklore around the song and cites many sources. It is the main source used here.","known":["What is documented: Banjo Paterson visited Dagworth Station, near Winton, in 1895. Christina Macpherson played a tune, and he wrote words to it. The first published version of the song was in 1902. Samuel Hoffmeister, called “Frenchy”, was found dead at a shearers’ camp at a billabong near Kynuna on 2 September 1894. That was the same day the Dagworth shearing shed was burned. An inquest three days later found that he died by suicide.","Why the link is doubtful: Wikipedia says claims that Paterson visited the Combo waterhole are not confirmed by Paterson or by others who were there. It says there is no evidence that he made the 52 kilometre round trip to the billabong where Hoffmeister died, and that he is unlikely to have had time. In 2010 a barrister who studied the inquest transcript gave the opinion that the death looked more like an assassination than a suicide. That opinion is also unproven. Historians also disagree about whether the strike inspired the song."],"src":["wikiMatilda"]},{"title":"Beyond the black stump","where":"Blackall, Queensland · 1887","text":["“Beyond the black stump” means a long way from towns. The people of Blackall say the expression began in their town. They say that in 1887 surveyors set up an astronomical survey station on a stump there. They say that the country to the west of Blackall was then described as beyond the black stump."],"from":"The Wikipedia article “Black stump” sets out the Blackall claim and two other claims. It lists early uses of the phrase from a New South Wales court case, a novel of 1888 and the Bulletin of 1900.","known":["What is documented: Wikipedia says an astro station was set up at Blackall in 1887 as part of a survey that fixed the position of towns from Brisbane to Boulia. A petrified stump now marks the place. The original blackened stump was destroyed by fire.","Why the claim is doubtful: Wikipedia says the origin of the expression is “contested”. At least three towns claim it: Blackall in Queensland, Coolah in New South Wales, and Merriwagga and Gunbar in New South Wales. The phrase “black stump” appears as a real tree stump used as a landmark in a New South Wales court case about a land boundary, in Rolf Boldrewood’s novel Robbery Under Arms (1888) and in the Bulletin in 1900. Wikipedia says it was “considered at the time” that country west of Blackall was beyond the black stump, but it does not quote anything written then. The Blackall claim is unproven."],"src":["wikiBlackStump"]},{"title":"The trees marked with an L","where":"Dawson River, Taroom · 1844 and 1845","text":["The explorer Ludwig Leichhardt travelled through the Dawson River country in 1844 on his way to Port Essington, near Darwin. People say he marked trees along the way with the letter L. The best known is a coolibah tree in Yaldwyn Street, Taroom, called the Leichhardt Tree. The story says the tree carries his mark."],"from":"The Wikipedia article “Leichhardt Tree, Taroom” is based on the tree’s listing on the Queensland Heritage Register.","known":["What is documented: Leichhardt’s party left Jimbour Station on 1 October 1844 and crossed the Dawson River where Taroom now stands. In mid-1845 Pemberton Hodgson, tracing Leichhardt’s track, found three large gum trees blazed with the letter L.","Why the link is doubtful: The article says Leichhardt is “thought to have” blazed the Taroom tree, and that the coolibah in Yaldwyn Street “may have been one of” the three trees Hodgson found. In 1916 one L and part of the date 1844 could still be seen. Bark then grew over part of the date and over the second L. In the mid-1970s the blaze could just be seen but not read. It is no longer visible, so it cannot be checked. A flood mark of 1893 and a surveyor’s mark have also been cut into the tree. The link between this tree and Leichhardt is believed but not proven."],"src":["wikiLeichhardtTree"]},{"title":"The banker whose coffin held only stones","where":"Brisbane · after 3 February 1896","text":["The story says that Edward Drury, the general manager of the Queensland National Bank, did not really die in 1896. After his death some sensational claims said he had faked his death, and that his coffin held only stones."],"from":"The Australian Dictionary of Biography entry for Drury mentions the story.","known":["What is documented: the Australian Dictionary of Biography says Drury was general manager of the bank from 1872 and died on 3 February 1896. Merrett and the RBA say that the bank was later found to be deeply insolvent, and the Government guaranteed its deposits in 1897.","Why the story is doubtful: the entry says the claims were never proved. This resource found no other source for the story, and did not read the sensational journals. The sources do not say why the story spread."],"src":["adbDrury","merrett","rba90"]}]},"folkTeach":[{"q":"The court report says the jury found Readford not guilty, but not why. How should a historian treat a story that gives a reason nobody recorded?","a":["A verdict is evidence of what the jury decided. It is not evidence of why. A story that supplies a reason, such as admiration, adds a guess where the record is silent.","Ask students to compare the number of cattle in the story (about 1,000) with the number in the charge (401) and suggest reasons for the difference without deciding which is right.","Ask what other sources could help, such as station records, the court file or later reminiscences, and how reliable each would be."]},{"q":"Why might people remember a bushranger as polite? Which details in this story could be checked against records?","a":["A polite outlaw makes a more interesting story. Newspapers and police reports of 1865 and 1866 could confirm the robberies, the capture and the sentence. The folded letters and the cheques are the kind of detail that a report of the time might mention, or might not.","The buried treasure cannot be tested from documents unless a record of it is found. Ask students what that means for how far the story can be trusted."]},{"q":"The song and the death of Hoffmeister are a year apart. What would you need to find to show that the song is about him?","a":["You would need a statement by Paterson, or by someone who was with him, saying the song was about Hoffmeister, written at the time or soon after. Wikipedia says no such confirmation exists for a visit to the billabong.","Ask students to separate three claims. First, Paterson wrote the words in 1895 in the Winton district, which is well supported. Second, the song is about Hoffmeister, which is unproven. Third, Hoffmeister did not die by suicide, which is also unproven.","This story includes a death by suicide. Handle it with care and let students step away if they need to."]},{"q":"Three towns each say the expression began with them. What kind of evidence would show which claim is right, if any?","a":["The best evidence is the earliest dated written use of the phrase, in a letter, a newspaper or an official record. Students can search Trove for “black stump” and sort the results by date.","Even the earliest written use may not show where the phrase began. A phrase can be used in many places before anyone writes it down. Discuss why “we cannot tell” is a valid conclusion."]},{"q":"The mark on the tree can no longer be read. What other evidence could show whether this is one of Leichhardt’s trees?","a":["Look for descriptions by people who saw the mark before it faded, such as the 1916 observations. Compare them with Hodgson’s description of three blazed trees in 1845. Compare the position of the tree with Leichhardt’s published journal of his camp at the Dawson crossing.","Ask students why a mark made by someone else, or a surveyor’s mark, could be mistaken for Leichhardt’s."]},{"q":"A bank fails and its manager dies. People say he is alive abroad and that his coffin holds stones. What would a historian need to see before treating this as more than a rumour?","a":["A death certificate, a burial record, a newspaper report of the funeral, or a statement by someone who saw the coffin would count as evidence. The source says the claims were never proved.","Ask students why a rumour like this might spread when many depositors lost money. The source does not say, so treat any answer as a guess, and ask how a historian could test it."]}],"london":{"lead":"Queensland’s runs were stocked and fenced with borrowed money. Some of it came from banks and companies based in London and Scotland, or from British savers who put money into them. This page follows that money from the 1860s to the Federation Drought, and says plainly what the sources do and do not show.","how":["Each company and bank on the list for this page was checked against sources for its actual part in Queensland. Some were barely in Queensland before the 1890s. Those findings are on this page too, so nobody has to guess.","Many of the best sources are histories of all of Australia. Where a figure is not for Queensland alone, the text says so.","Some source pages blocked automated fetching or timed out. Where that happened the source note says the page was read as a search-tool excerpt. Those facts need a check against the full page.","No source read says what London investors knew about how the land had been taken. That gap is listed at the end of the page."],"sections":[{"h":"Why a run needed borrowed money","p":["A person could not lease a run until it was stocked. Wallis and Burke write that, in the Burke district, people who wanted a run were obliged to stock the land before they could apply to lease it from the government. Stock, fencing and wages had to be paid for before the first wool was sold.","The Queensland Government’s own review of 1909 says it was hard to get produce to port from remote stations. It says one wool clip was often kept on a station until the next year’s shearing had begun. So a squatter usually had to give a lien on the clip. A lien is a claim on the wool, so the lender is paid from it first. The review says the interest rate, including the agent’s commission, was commonly 12 per cent a year.","The Liens on Wool Act was one of the laws of the first session of the Queensland Parliament, which closed in September 1860. The 1909 review says it also covered mortgages on sheep, cattle and horses. A stock mortgage lets a lender claim the animals if the loan is not repaid."],"src":["atfBurke","ofhc"]},{"h":"The agency houses and pastoral companies","p":["An agency house, or stock and station agent, sold a squatter’s wool and supplied goods. Many also lent money. The Australian Dictionary of Biography says Frederick Dalgety built a London-linked business that gave wool-growers finance, transport, storage, insurance, technical advice and wool sales. It says that Britain provided both the market and the long-term capital.","Merrett explains the wider system for all of Australia. Banks lent to specialist companies such as pastoral finance companies. Those companies also took deposits and sold debentures, which are written promises to repay a loan with interest. They lent that money on to the pastoral industry.","Merrett quotes an estimate from the Australasian Insurance and Banking Record. It says British investors had put £18 million into the shares of pastoral companies and £28 million into their debentures, and that British insurance companies had lent another £7 million on mortgages over squatting properties. These figures are for all of Australia, not Queensland alone, and they are an estimate from a journal of the time."],"src":["adbDalgety","merrett"]},{"h":"Owners who lived somewhere else","p":["Peter Woodley writes that by 1890 Queensland’s properties were enormous. The largest ran 375,000 sheep, and they were generally owned by pastoral and finance companies. He says the owners of these large enterprises often lived elsewhere.","He writes that many of the entrepreneurs with Queensland pastoral interests had Melbourne connections, because since the 1860s Melbourne had been home to banks and pastoral companies with assets in the northern colony. He names the Fairbairn family as an example. George Fairbairn the younger managed several Queensland stations and became general manager of the family’s businesses and managing director of the United Mortgage and Agency Company of Australia Limited. Woodley says the historian Stephen Svensen estimates that the Fairbairns owned an interest in one sheep in every five in Queensland.","The Queensland Historical Atlas says much of the expansion of the 1860s came largely from Victorian wealth invested in Queensland runs. Wallis and Burke say the first pastoralists in the Burke district were often backed by finance from the big cities. They say David McConnel’s Cressbrook, taken up in the Brisbane Valley in 1841, had substantial family funding from England, and that interest in the Warrego became apparent in the 1870s when parties from Melbourne began investing."],"src":["woodley","qhPast","atfBurke","atfMoreton","atfWarrego"]},{"h":"Case study: Bowen Downs and the Scottish Australian Investment Company","p":["William Landsborough and Nat Buchanan found the country at Bowen Downs. The James Cook University archive says the Scottish-Australian Company provided the capital for the station. Wallis and Burke say the station was on the lands of the Iningai people and that its first cattle arrived in October 1862.","The local history says the partners took up the country as the Landsborough River Company. It says the Scottish Australian Company, represented by Morehead and Young, held half the shares, Landsborough a quarter, and Nat Buchanan and Edward Cornish an eighth each. Wikipedia says that after a drought Buchanan left the station in 1867 and gave his eighth share to Morehead.","The local history says Bowen Downs eventually covered 3,683 square miles. It says the company set up Mt Cornish as a separate cattle station in 1872 and sold it in 1904 to recover some of its losses. The records at James Cook University include wage ledgers for shearers and other men from 1872.","Wallis and Burke also say the Landsborough River Company, including Morehead and Young of the Scottish Australian Investment Company, had stocked Beamesbrook, near Burketown in the Burke district, by 1865."],"src":["jcuBowen","atfMitchell","muttaburra","wikiBowen","atfBurke"],"flag":"Sources disagree about the first manager of Bowen Downs. The James Cook University archive and Wikipedia say Nat Buchanan. Wallis and Burke say Alexander “Long” Gordon. They also disagree on its early stock and size: Wikipedia gives 3,000 cattle and 1,500 square miles, and the local history gives 5,000 cattle in October 1862, with more cattle and 25,000 sheep added soon after.","image":"bowenSheds"},{"h":"When the lender took the run","p":["The best-documented example from the 1860s is John Douglas, who later became Premier. Jeremy Hodes writes that Douglas bought Tooloombah station in 1860 and had to take out a £5,000 mortgage with the Sydney firm Gilchrist, Watt and Company to pay for it. The panic of 1866 hurt his finances. In 1867 he was forced to transfer Tooloombah to the firm, which financed its purchase by the Beardmore brothers the next year. The sale did not fully clear his debt, and he was declared insolvent in 1872.","Hodes quotes a squatter who wrote that the panic of 1866 “played the mischief with all pastoralists who were either largely in debt or whose credit was not good”. He also quotes James Taylor, a squatter and member of parliament, saying in 1868 that “Ninety-nine out of every hundred squatters in Queensland were insolvent.” That was a politician’s statement in a debate, not a count.","Wallis and Burke give another case. In the Kennedy district the run Inkerman was taken over by Robert Towns by mortgage in 1864.","In the 1890s the same thing happened on a larger scale. The Queensland Historical Atlas says the Australian Mercantile Land and Finance Company held 80 stations as security for loans by 1904, after taking foreclosed land in the 1890s. Six of them were in Queensland. To foreclose is to take the property because a loan was not repaid. The National Museum of Australia says banks foreclosed on some stations in the Federation Drought. The Queensland National Bank held pastoral stations too. The Queensland National Pastoral Company was set up in 1915 to acquire its station interests."],"src":["hodesDouglas","atfKennedy","qhAMLF","nma","eoasQNPC"]},{"h":"The 1866 financial crisis","p":["Hodes sets out what happened. From separation in 1859 Queensland governments spent more than they earned, mostly on railways. In 1864 parliament approved a loan of over £1 million and asked the Union Bank of Australia to place the debentures. The bank placed only £300,000 worth by the end of 1865, and it had made large advances to the Government against the unsold bonds. In May 1866 a new loan of £1,170,950 was authorised. The Sydney agents of the Agra and Masterman’s Bank offered to place £500,000 of it in London, and the Union Bank agreed to advance another £100,000.","The London firm Overend, Gurney and Company failed. Wikipedia says it closed on 10 May 1866 and that Agra and Masterman’s Bank suspended payment on 6 June 1866 after the panic that began then. Hodes writes that the news reached Brisbane on 10 July 1866. The Government was told that the new loan could not go ahead. The Union Bank treated its extra £100,000 as cancelled and disallowed government cheques.","Wikipedia says the Bank of Queensland, which was set up in London and opened in Brisbane in 1863, suspended payment. Its London board said a large part of its capital had been lost on advances against sheep and cattle stations, sawmills and even newspaper proprietors.","The Premier, Arthur Macalister, said the Government would issue unsecured government notes. Governor Bowen refused to sanction them, and Macalister’s ministry resigned on 18 July. A bill for £300,000 of Treasury bills at 10 per cent interest passed on 20 July. Hodes says that on 22 July a telegram said Agra and Masterman’s could now carry out its agreement, and that local banks and the Treasury bills eased the crisis.","Even so, public works stopped, including the railway from Ipswich to Toowoomba and the new Parliament House. There were 156 bankruptcies that year. Immigration stopped and unemployed workers left for the southern colonies. Hodes says the recession ended after gold was found at Gympie late in 1867. He adds that for the pastoral industry, already hurt by drought, prices and profits did not recover until the early 1870s."],"src":["hodes1866","agraWiki","boq"],"flag":"The sources differ on the details. Wikipedia’s page on Agra and Masterman’s says it suspended on 6 June 1866, but Hodes says the bank was “involved in” the failure of Overend Gurney and that it sent a telegram on 22 July saying it could carry out the agreement after all. Wikipedia’s page on the Bank of Queensland puts the Overend Gurney collapse in July 1866, but its page on Agra and Masterman’s says 10 May. This resource uses 10 May for the London failure and 10 July for the news in Brisbane."},{"h":"The 1880s land boom","p":["The 1909 Government review describes the 1880s. It says money from outside Queensland came in through financial institutions at lower rates of interest. It says prices in many cases doubled and quadrupled in a few months “without much regard to the income-earning power”, and that demand for real property almost stopped in the second half of 1888. It adds that from then until 1893 values were as far as possible held up by the mortgagees, because they believed the stagnation must be temporary. A mortgagee is a lender who holds a mortgage.","Merrett’s figures show how much lending grew. In Queensland, bank advances were 35.6 per cent of the colony’s output in 1880 and 91.6 per cent in 1892. He says Queensland’s output peaked in 1888 and bank advances peaked in 1889. Woodley writes that in the 1880s the number of sheep in Queensland more than doubled, and the Queensland Historical Atlas says sheep numbers peaked at 21 million by 1892.","Some companies were new in Queensland in these years. The New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency Company opened branches in Brisbane and Rockhampton in 1884. Dalgety and Company Limited was registered in London that year, but its first Queensland branch, at Rockhampton, opened only in 1891. The Muttaburra local history says the first large land resumptions at Bowen Downs were in 1885 to 1887, in a decade of droughts and falling wool prices."],"src":["ofhc","merrett","woodley","qhPast","anuNZLM","anuDalgety","muttaburra"]},{"h":"The 1893 bank failures","p":["The RBA says the Queensland National Bank, the Bank of North Queensland and the Royal Bank of Queensland suspended payment within three days of each other in mid May 1893. Together they held just over half of Queensland’s banking assets. To suspend payment means to stop paying depositors on demand.","Scottish money mattered. The RBA says a run by Scottish depositors in May 1893 prompted the Queensland National Bank to suspend. Merrett says British deposits were about 40 per cent of the total in Australian banks, and that much of them matured at the end of the Scottish Whitsun term in mid May. The RBA also says that in April 1892 the proceeds of Queensland Government loans raised in London were used to meet depositors’ withdrawals from the same bank.","The bank had close links with the Government. The RBA says Thomas McIlwraith was on its board for five years before he became Premier in 1879, and that the bank was awarded all the Queensland Government’s business that year. The Australian Dictionary of Biography says its general manager, Edward Drury, lent £328,000 to McIlwraith and £67,000 to himself.","The Government agreed to a reconstruction after its auditor reported that the bank was solvent. Under the reconstruction, £2 million of the Government’s own deposits was locked up for between 6 and 12 years. The RBA says the bank’s losses were hidden by accounting fraud in 1894 and 1895. In 1897 a government-appointed committee found the bank deeply insolvent. The Government then guaranteed all its deposits for a year. The RBA says the Government was fully repaid in 1918.","Other firms were hurt. The New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency Company suspended business in July 1893 and was reconstructed in 1894. Burns Philp lost two of its three banks, the Queensland National Bank and the Bank of North Queensland. Merrett says that if the whole system had failed, only a handful of banks might have survived, with the Bank of New South Wales, the Union Bank of Australia and the Bank of Australasia as the contenders.","Merrett says Queensland’s output peaked in 1888, bottomed in 1891 and was back at its earlier peak by 1894, much sooner than in Victoria. But he says Queensland’s bank advances did not recover their 1889 level until 1916."],"src":["rba90","adbDrury","merrett","anuNZLM","anuBurnsPhilp"],"image":"qnb"},{"h":"The 1891 shearers’ strike: how it links to the money","p":["The links the sources show are about who owned and led, not about orders from a bank. The Queensland State Archives says that in late 1890, as the wool price fell, employers drastically cut wages and entitlements for shearers. Woodley says the Queensland Pastoral Employers’ Association formed at Barcaldine in April 1889, and that pastoralists met in Melbourne and Sydney in November and December 1890 to form the Pastoralists’ Union of Australia.","Woodley says entrepreneurs with Queensland interests rarely feature in accounts of the strike. Frank Murphy, an owner of extensive Queensland sheep stations and the member for Barcoo, became president of the Queensland association. George Fairbairn the younger, the managing director of the United Mortgage and Agency Company, called a meeting of the Queensland delegates before the Melbourne conference. Thomas McIlwraith, whom Woodley calls a wealthy pastoralist and financier and a member of the association, was Treasurer in 1891. He told the unions that the pastoralists were ready to meet only if the bush workers first accepted “freedom of contract”.","Woodley writes that by April at least nine thousand men were on strike. He also says Aboriginal shearers were active unionists by the early 1890s and took part in the strike. He cites the historian Jordan Humphreys for this.","No source read for this page says a London bank or company ordered the wage cuts. The link is that the owners, finance company managers and employers’ leaders overlapped."],"src":["qsaStrike","woodley"],"flag":"Sources differ on how many strike leaders were jailed. The State Archives blog says thirteen organisers were arrested in March and sent to St Helena for three years, and also says 21 men boarded the boat on 26 May. Woodley says fourteen men were tried at Rockhampton in May 1891 and twelve were convicted and sentenced to three years. The Timeline entry for the strike in the main resource gives thirteen."},{"h":"The Federation Drought and the companies","p":["The National Museum of Australia says the drought of 1895 to 1903 was the worst in Australia’s history measured by stock losses, that banks foreclosed on some stations, and that large stations were divided into smaller holdings.","Bowen Downs shows what this meant on one company’s runs. The Muttaburra local history says the station was badly overstocked after land resumptions. It says that by 1902, 276,244 sheep had died at Bowen Downs and 134,000 remained, and that at Mt Cornish 2,000 head of cattle remained out of 27,417, with 644 horses dead. It says the Scottish Australian Company sold Mt Cornish in 1904 to recover some of its losses.","The same local history says the Labour Government increased pastoral rents by as much as 100 per cent in 1902."],"src":["nma","muttaburra"],"flag":"The 1902 rent claim in the local history does not match the Queensland State Archives account used elsewhere in this resource, which says the Land Act of 1902 was a Land Act for lessees. The local history has no footnotes, and its claim is not used anywhere else here. Please check it against the Act."}],"checks":[{"name":"Scottish Australian Investment Company","verdict":"Yes, from 1861 to 1862","text":"It financed Bowen Downs, with first cattle in October 1862, and Wallis and Burke say its partners had stocked Beamesbrook in the Burke district by 1865. The four other stations in its Queensland archive, Mt Emu Plains, Kynuna, Albion Downs and Lucknow, joined its holdings only in the early 1950s, so they are not part of this story.","src":["jcuBowen","jcuScot","atfMitchell","atfBurke","anuSAIC"]},{"name":"Union Bank of Australia","verdict":"Yes, from 1858","text":"It was founded in London in 1837 and opened a Brisbane branch in 1858. In 1864 parliament asked it to place the Queensland Government’s loan, and in 1866 the Government owed it nearly £500,000.","src":["unionWiki","hodes1866"]},{"name":"Bank of New South Wales","verdict":"Yes, from 1850. Not a London bank","text":"It opened its Brisbane branch in November 1850 and was one of the four banks in Queensland by late 1862. It was based in Sydney, not London. No source read confirms the idea that it was a London bank.","src":["slqBNSW","boq"]},{"name":"Bank of Australasia","verdict":"Yes, from 1853. Pastoral lending not shown","text":"It opened a branch at Ipswich on 7 November 1853 and was one of the four banks in Queensland by late 1862. No source read shows how much it lent to Queensland pastoralists.","src":["qutBoA","boq"]},{"name":"Queensland National Bank","verdict":"Yes, but set up in Brisbane, with British depositors","text":"It was set up in Brisbane in 1872 by Queensland businessmen. It had a London branch by 1914, and Scottish depositors in 1893. It held the Government’s account from 1879 and suspended payment in May 1893. Pastoral stations ended up with the bank.","src":["qldHeritageQNB","wikiQNB","rba90","eoasQNPC"]},{"name":"Dalgety and Company","verdict":"Not in Queensland until 1891","text":"Dalgety built its business in Victoria, New Zealand and Sydney. The company was registered in London in 1884, and its first Queensland branch opened at Rockhampton in 1891, then Brisbane in 1894. No source read shows a Dalgety branch in Queensland in the 1860s to 1880s.","src":["adbDalgety","anuDalgety"]},{"name":"Goldsbrough Mort and Company","verdict":"Partly shown: 1890","text":"The company was formed in 1888. A catalogue entry lists a letter from its Rockhampton manager asking for further advances in June 1890. I could not confirm what Mort and Company or Goldsbrough did in Queensland before that.","src":["anuGM"]},{"name":"Australian Mercantile Land and Finance Company","verdict":"Brief in the 1860s; small until the 1900s","text":"It was registered in England in 1863 and opened in Brisbane that year. It moved its business to Victoria in 1865. By 1904 it held 80 stations as security for loans, six of them in Queensland, and it bought its first Queensland station, Retro, in 1906.","src":["anuAMLF","qhAMLF"]},{"name":"New Zealand Loan and Mercantile Agency Company","verdict":"Yes, from 1884","text":"It was registered in England in 1865 but opened in Brisbane and Rockhampton only in 1884. It suspended business in July 1893 and was reconstructed in 1894.","src":["anuNZLM"]},{"name":"North British Australasian Company","verdict":"Partly shown: before 1860","text":"The only Queensland evidence read is a short excerpt of a biography. It says John Deuchar worked for the company from 1842 to 1855 and managed Goomburra and Rosenthal. I found nothing on its part in the 1860s to 1890s.","src":["adbDeuchar"]},{"name":"Bank of Queensland (1863–1866)","verdict":"Yes, a London-formed bank that failed in 1866","text":"It was not on the original list, but the sources show it was formed in London, opened in Brisbane in August 1863 and suspended payment in 1866. Its London board said it had lost a large part of its capital on advances against stations and other securities.","src":["boq"]},{"name":"United Mortgage and Agency Company of Australia","verdict":"Yes, through the Fairbairn family, by 1890","text":"Woodley names George Fairbairn the younger as its managing director and says the Fairbairns owned an interest in about one in five Queensland sheep. I did not find when the company began lending in Queensland.","src":["woodley"]},{"name":"Other names","verdict":"Not checked in depth","text":"The Australian Joint Stock Bank was one of the four banks in Queensland by late 1862, according to Wikipedia. Gilchrist, Watt and Company, a Sydney firm, held John Douglas’s mortgage. I did not read enough about other firms to say more.","src":["boq","hodesDouglas"]}],"means":[{"h":"For Aboriginal people","p":["Woodley writes that by the 1880s graziers were moving into the Mitchell, Maranoa and Warrego districts, and that this was accomplished through extensive violence and disruption inflicted on Aboriginal people with the aid of a Native Mounted Police force directed by white officers. The district pages used for the map give earlier first-run dates for these districts, in the 1840s and 1860s. Wallis and Burke say Aboriginal resistance in the Mitchell district was defeated less than two decades after it opened for white occupation.","On Bowen Downs, the station financed by the Scottish Australian Investment Company, Wallis and Burke name Alexander “Long” Gordon as the first manager. They say he was responsible for the mass killing of 13 Aboriginal people in mid-1866, in revenge for the killing of a white man some weeks earlier. They also say the Aramac manager of that name is thought to have massacred at least 13 Iningai-speaking people in mid-1866. Other sources name Nat Buchanan as the first manager, so the sources disagree on who managed the station. The sources read do not say what the company’s London or Scottish owners knew.","Woodley also writes that Traditional Owners kept negotiating access to Country and kept working for the pastoral industry, but under exploitative conditions. Wallis and Burke say the station owner James Allan, in the Mitchell district, used Aboriginal labour on his stations. By the early 1890s many Aboriginal shearers were union members.","When a run was mortgaged, the lender’s security was land that had been taken from its Traditional Owners. The sources read do not say whether the lenders knew or cared. That question is unanswered here."],"src":["woodley","atfMitchell","jcuBowen"]},{"h":"For workers","p":["Woodley says machine shearing involved considerable investment by station owners and made conflict worse. The Queensland State Archives says wages and entitlements were cut in late 1890 as the wool price fell. Woodley says that as wool prices fell and the depression deepened in the years after the strike, bush workers’ bargaining power only weakened.","Woodley also writes that, for some workers, excluding Chinese people and South Sea Islanders was a fundamental plank of their unionism, and that in places Chinese businesses were menaced during the strike. This is part of the record of who the workers were and what they asked for."],"src":["woodley","qsaStrike"]},{"h":"For selectors","p":["The Queensland State Archives summary of the land laws says the Act of 1868 let selectors choose land in settled districts that was not under pastoral lease, and that the Act of 1884 abolished pre-emptive purchases by pastoralists. The existing Land laws pages of this resource cover the rest.","I did not find a source that shows how banks and finance companies treated selectors as a group, or how many selectors lost their land through debt."],"src":["qsaLand"]}],"gaps":["How many Queensland runs were held by London-linked lenders in any one year. No source read gives a Queensland count for the 1860s to 1880s.","What Goldsbrough Mort, Mort and Company, and the North British Australasian Company did in Queensland before 1890.","The pastoral lending of the Bank of Australasia and the Bank of New South Wales in Queensland.","What investors in London or Scotland knew about how the land was taken, and what they thought about it.","How lenders treated selectors, and how many selectors lost land through debt.","A source that says a bank or company ordered the wage cuts of 1890 to 1891. None was found.","Newspaper reports of 1866 and 1893. They could not be searched here, so the newspapers are read only as quoted by historians."]},"settlement":{"outline":[[[138,-16.54],[138.29,-16.75],[138.66,-16.78],[139.01,-16.9],[139.16,-17.03],[139.15,-17.17],[139.24,-17.32],[139.44,-17.38],[140,-17.71],[140.51,-17.64],[140.85,-17.44],[140.95,-17.02],[141.29,-16.5],[141.43,-16.06],[141.38,-15.93],[141.44,-15.65],[141.66,-15.09],[141.52,-14.48],[141.6,-14.11],[141.47,-13.87],[141.53,-13.57],[141.69,-13.25],[141.58,-12.99],[141.75,-12.82],[141.81,-12.67],[141.94,-12.91],[141.91,-12.76],[142.01,-12.71],[141.82,-12.65],[141.97,-12.59],[141.8,-12.58],[141.69,-12.5],[141.74,-12.45],[141.66,-12.45],[141.7,-12.54],[141.59,-12.54],[141.72,-12.23],[141.81,-12.24],[141.75,-12.21],[141.83,-12.01],[141.91,-11.95],[141.92,-12.11],[142.03,-12.04],[141.95,-11.9],[142.13,-11.34],[142.15,-10.95],[142.32,-10.9],[142.45,-10.71],[142.55,-10.69],[142.62,-10.75],[142.5,-10.95],[142.59,-10.86],[142.67,-11],[142.74,-10.98],[142.87,-11.38],[142.82,-11.48],[142.86,-11.84],[142.99,-11.93],[143.1,-11.9],[143.25,-11.97],[143.09,-12.15],[143.08,-12.33],[143.25,-12.39],[143.29,-12.53],[143.43,-12.61],[143.36,-12.88],[143.54,-12.83],[143.5,-13.08],[143.6,-13.52],[143.53,-13.75],[143.69,-14.01],[143.78,-14.4],[143.94,-14.51],[144.16,-14.37],[144.2,-14.25],[144.3,-14.31],[144.49,-14.16],[144.58,-14.26],[144.58,-14.36],[144.63,-14.34],[144.62,-14.47],[144.68,-14.56],[144.9,-14.61],[145.03,-14.81],[145.22,-14.83],[145.28,-14.95],[145.35,-14.94],[145.24,-15.17],[145.27,-15.25],[145.35,-15.25],[145.24,-15.43],[145.37,-15.75],[145.36,-15.9],[145.46,-16.06],[145.4,-16.44],[145.72,-16.8],[145.77,-16.99],[145.84,-16.88],[145.96,-16.87],[145.88,-17.06],[146.15,-17.63],[146.01,-18.24],[146.18,-18.38],[146.21,-18.51],[146.34,-18.53],[146.28,-18.87],[146.46,-19.07],[146.75,-19.17],[146.91,-19.3],[147.01,-19.25],[147.02,-19.18],[147.13,-19.41],[147.42,-19.41],[147.42,-19.31],[147.56,-19.54],[147.57,-19.71],[147.68,-19.83],[147.79,-19.84],[147.75,-19.69],[147.82,-19.71],[147.93,-19.9],[148.07,-19.88],[148.28,-19.97],[148.26,-20.08],[148.4,-20.2],[148.46,-20.19],[148.44,-20.06],[148.56,-20.05],[148.61,-20.21],[148.65,-20.16],[148.68,-20.27],[148.78,-20.24],[148.94,-20.53],[148.81,-20.43],[148.7,-20.44],[148.72,-20.5],[148.66,-20.57],[148.84,-20.78],[148.83,-20.89],[148.88,-20.84],[149.06,-20.9],[149.03,-20.98],[149.15,-21],[149.23,-21.09],[149.2,-21.22],[149.29,-21.28],[149.29,-21.4],[149.34,-21.42],[149.29,-21.51],[149.4,-21.5],[149.43,-21.58],[149.49,-21.54],[149.44,-21.79],[149.59,-22.28],[149.7,-22.34],[149.66,-22.49],[149.81,-22.38],[150.05,-22.65],[149.92,-22.3],[150.05,-22.14],[150.19,-22.38],[150.61,-22.61],[150.53,-22.43],[150.57,-22.32],[150.76,-22.46],[150.67,-22.52],[150.73,-22.63],[150.77,-22.53],[150.83,-22.7],[150.79,-22.93],[150.74,-22.93],[150.76,-23.16],[150.84,-23.25],[150.79,-23.31],[150.88,-23.49],[150.81,-23.51],[151.01,-23.57],[151.19,-23.8],[151.54,-24.02],[151.55,-24.09],[151.58,-24.04],[151.69,-24.1],[151.64,-23.98],[151.72,-24.06],[151.78,-24.02],[152.12,-24.59],[152.47,-24.81],[152.51,-25],[152.64,-25.16],[152.57,-25.18],[152.91,-25.29],[152.93,-25.42],[152.87,-25.48],[152.96,-25.44],[152.97,-25.5],[152.88,-25.67],[153.02,-25.83],[153.02,-25.97],[153.06,-25.82],[153.12,-25.93],[153.21,-25.93],[153.07,-26.32],[153.14,-26.39],[153.11,-26.65],[153.21,-27.08],[153.08,-27.11],[153.04,-27.19],[153.12,-27.2],[153.06,-27.31],[153.17,-27.36],[153.4,-27.81],[153.45,-27.79],[153.4,-27.95],[153.51,-28.15],[153.35,-28.25],[153.19,-28.26],[153.11,-28.35],[152.75,-28.36],[152.62,-28.29],[152.56,-28.33],[152.49,-28.26],[152.39,-28.37],[151.96,-28.54],[152.07,-28.7],[152.01,-28.9],[151.86,-28.92],[151.82,-28.97],[151.73,-28.89],[151.56,-28.95],[151.38,-29.17],[151.29,-28.92],[151.06,-28.83],[151.05,-28.73],[150.79,-28.64],[150.45,-28.65],[150.3,-28.53],[149.7,-28.63],[149.48,-28.58],[149.42,-28.68],[149.09,-28.85],[148.97,-29],[141,-29],[141,-26],[138,-26],[138,-16.54]],[[153.37,-24.99],[153.06,-25.8],[152.95,-25.55],[153.07,-25.33],[153.02,-25.2],[153.24,-24.99],[153.14,-24.82],[153.28,-24.69],[153.28,-24.88],[153.37,-24.99]],[[139.73,-16.44],[139.71,-16.53],[139.49,-16.52],[139.52,-16.56],[139.47,-16.56],[139.44,-16.66],[139.3,-16.72],[139.18,-16.66],[139.14,-16.76],[139.16,-16.62],[139.29,-16.47],[139.58,-16.4],[139.73,-16.44]]],"marks":[{"name":"Barcaldine","year":1886,"lon":145.3,"lat":-23.55,"dx":12,"dy":16,"anchor":"start","note":"The railway reached Barcaldine in 1886.","src":["muttaburra"]},{"name":"Longreach","year":1892,"lon":144.25,"lat":-23.44,"dx":-10,"dy":18,"anchor":"end","note":"The railway reached Longreach in 1892.","src":["muttaburra"]}],"credit":"Outline: Natural Earth (public domain), simplified. Positions of districts are approximate.","lead":"This map shows how pastoral settlement spread north and west across what became Queensland, one decade at a time, from the 1840s to the 1890s. Use the slider or the Next and Back buttons to change the decade. Choose “List view” for the same information as text.","line":"Each new district brought runs, roads, ports and towns, and each one took more Country from its Traditional Owners.","lineNote":"This line is a summary written for this resource. Each district entry below gives the sources for its own parts.","how":["The dots mark the approximate middle of each pastoral district. They do not show district boundaries, and the outline of Queensland is simplified.","The “first runs” year is when the sources say squatters first took up a run. The “gazetted” year is when the Government officially named the district. These can be years apart.","Red dots are districts whose first runs are in the chosen decade. Green dots are districts that opened earlier. A grey diamond marks a railway reaching a town, as the sources date it.","The Country notes say which Traditional Owner groups the sources name. Many districts hold the Country of more than one people. Where a source names only some of them, this page says that others are not named. Spellings vary between sources."],"steps":[{"decade":1840,"label":"1840s","text":"Squatters took up the Darling Downs from March 1840. The first run in the Brisbane Valley was taken up on 15 July 1841, and Moreton was declared a pastoral district on 5 May 1842. In 1843 runs were taken up at Tiaro in Wide Bay and at Burrandowan in the Burnett. Maranoa and the southern Leichhardt were reached in the late 1840s.","src":["atf","atfMoreton","atfWideBay","atfMaranoa","atfLeichhardt"]},{"decade":1850,"label":"1850s","text":"The Archer brothers took up Gracemere in 1853, and Port Curtis and Leichhardt were declared on 10 January 1854. Gladstone was the first permanent settlement in Port Curtis in 1854, and Rockhampton followed in 1856. Queensland became a separate colony in December 1859. The Kennedy district was proclaimed at the end of 1859.","src":["atfPortCurtis","atfLeichhardt","atfKennedy"]},{"decade":1860,"label":"1860s","text":"Squatters moved far north and west. The first Kennedy runs and Joseph Fleming’s Warrego runs date from 1861. In early 1862 James Allan and Ernest Davis took up runs in the Mitchell, and Bowen Downs received its first cattle in October 1862. The Warrego was gazetted in 1864 and Burke in 1865. The first Gregory runs date from the mid-1860s. The financial crisis of 1866 followed.","src":["atfKennedy","atfWarrego","atfMitchell","atfBurke","atfGregoryN","atfGregoryS"]},{"decade":1870,"label":"1870s","text":"No new district opened on this map in the 1870s. Gregory North and Gregory South were gazetted in 1873. Wallis and Burke say interest in the Warrego became apparent only in the 1870s, when parties from Melbourne began investing. The Scottish Australian Company set up Mt Cornish in 1872.","src":["atfIndex","atfWarrego","muttaburra"]},{"decade":1880,"label":"1880s","text":"No new district opened on this map in the 1880s. By 1885 all the available land in Gregory South had been claimed. Woodley says the number of sheep in Queensland more than doubled in the 1880s. The Queensland Historical Atlas says artesian water was tapped after 1880. The railway reached Barcaldine in 1886, and the first large resumptions at Bowen Downs came in 1885 to 1887.","src":["atfGregoryS","woodley","qhPast","muttaburra"]},{"decade":1890,"label":"1890s","text":"The railway reached Longreach in 1892, and Queensland’s sheep numbers peaked at 21 million by 1892. The Federation Drought began in 1895. The Muttaburra local history says the first artesian bores were put down at Bowen Downs in this decade, and that its record number of sheep were shorn in 1899.","src":["muttaburra","qhPast","nma"]}],"districts":[{"id":"downs","name":"Darling Downs","label":"Darling Downs","from":1840,"first":"1840","gazetted":"1843","lon":151.3,"lat":-27.7,"dx":-8,"dy":4,"anchor":"end","note":"The Leslie brothers took up runs from March 1840. The earliest licensed runs include Gowrie, Eton Vale and Tummaville in 1841. The district was gazetted in 1843. Wallis and Burke count 92 conflict events here, with the most in 1848.","country":"The Keinjan, Giabal, Jarowair and Barunggam peoples, according to the State Library of Queensland.","src":["atf","atfIndex","slqShepherd"]},{"id":"moreton","name":"Moreton","from":1841,"first":"1841","gazetted":"1842","lon":152.3,"lat":-27,"dx":8,"dy":-6,"anchor":"start","note":"Cressbrook, widely credited as the first run in the Brisbane Valley, was taken up on 15 July 1841 by David McConnel, with substantial family funding from England. The district was declared on 5 May 1842, and 18 runs were taken up within six years. The poisoning at Kilcoy was in 1842.","country":"Wallis and Burke name the Kabi Kabi and Joondoburri peoples on Bribie Island and the Gubbi Gubbi people at Kilcoy. The Kabi Kabi Peoples Aboriginal Corporation describes Kabi Kabi Country from the Pine River near Redcliffe to the Isis River near Hervey Bay. Other peoples hold Country in this district too, but the sources read for this page do not name them.","src":["atfMoreton","atfIndex","kkpac"]},{"id":"burnett","name":"Burnett","from":1843,"first":"1843","gazetted":"1848","lon":151.4,"lat":-25.8,"dx":-8,"dy":4,"anchor":"end","note":"Henry Stuart Russell and William Glover took up Burrandowan in the south Burnett in 1843. The Archer brothers had Eidsvold and Coonumbula in the Burnett before they moved to Port Curtis. Wallis and Burke say the Burnett and Wide Bay were declared in 1848.","country":"The Wide Bay Burnett regional list names the Taribelang Bunda, Gooreng Gooreng, Gurang, Wakka Wakka and Wulli Wulli peoples, among others, but does not say which belongs to which district. Wallis and Burke say Cracow, west of Eidsvold, is on the lands of the Gurang Gurang, Wakka Wakka and Wulli Wulli peoples.","src":["atfWideBay","atfPortCurtis","atfLeichhardt","rdaWBB"]},{"id":"widebay","name":"Wide Bay","from":1843,"first":"1843","gazetted":"1847 or 1848","lon":152.6,"lat":-25.7,"dx":8,"dy":12,"anchor":"start","note":"John Eales established Noomoowoolloo at Tiaro in 1843, the first run in Wide Bay. At first Wide Bay reached north to near where Rockhampton was later built. Its northern half became part of Port Curtis in 1854.","country":"The Kabi Kabi Peoples Aboriginal Corporation says Kabi Kabi Country includes the Mary River and reaches to the Isis River near Hervey Bay. The Wide Bay Burnett regional list also names the Butchulla and other peoples, without saying which district each belongs to.","src":["atfWideBay","kkpac","rdaWBB"],"flag":"Wallis and Burke’s table gives 1847 for Wide Bay, but their text says both Wide Bay and the Burnett were declared in 1848."},{"id":"maranoa","name":"Maranoa","from":1847,"first":"about 1847 to 1848","gazetted":"1848","lon":148.8,"lat":-26.6,"dx":-8,"dy":4,"anchor":"end","note":"Within a year of Thomas Mitchell’s expedition of 1846, Allan MacPherson took up Mount Abundance. James Blyth’s Tingun run dates from about 1848. MacPherson left in 1849 because of the level of frontier conflict. The district was declared open in late 1848, and Surat was established by mid-1850. Wallis and Burke list a book called “Goodbye Bussamarai: The Mandandanji Land War, Southern Queensland 1842–1852” (Collins, 2002).","country":"The Mandandanji and Gunggari peoples are named as the traditional owners of the Maranoa Regional Council area. The Gunggari corporation says Mandandanji Country lies east of Gunggari Country and Kooma Country to the south, and gives variant names for the Gunggari culture.","src":["atfMaranoa","maranoaProfile","gunggari"]},{"id":"leichhardt","name":"Leichhardt","from":1848,"first":"late 1840s","gazetted":"1854","lon":148.4,"lat":-24.3,"dx":-8,"dy":4,"anchor":"end","note":"Squatters following Leichhardt’s route took up Taroom and Juandah in the late 1840s and runs along the Dawson River. Cracow was first taken up in 1851. The district was declared open on 10 January 1854, although squatters had taken up runs in the south much earlier. The Cullin-la-ringo killings were in 1861 near Springsure.","country":"Springsure and Cullin-la-ringo are described as Gayiri, Bidjara and Ghungalu Country in the Places page of this resource. Wallis and Burke say Cracow is on the lands of the Gurang Gurang, Wakka Wakka and Wulli Wulli peoples.","src":["atfLeichhardt","atfIndex","guardian","newcastle"]},{"id":"portcurtis","name":"Port Curtis","from":1853,"first":"1853","gazetted":"1854","lon":150.4,"lat":-23.7,"dx":8,"dy":12,"anchor":"start","note":"The Archer brothers took up Gracemere in 1853, and the Leith Hay brothers took up Rannes in April 1853. The district was proclaimed on 10 January 1854. Gladstone was the first permanent settlement in 1854, and Rockhampton followed in 1856. Wallis and Burke say much of the land was taken by “run hunters” or speculators who often sold it quickly.","country":"Wallis and Burke name the Bailai, Gurang, Gooreng Gooreng and Taribelang people in an account of 1802 on the coast. Darumbal Enterprises describes Rockhampton and Gracemere as Darumbal Country.","src":["atfPortCurtis","fitzroy","darumbal"]},{"id":"kennedy","name":"Kennedy","from":1861,"first":"1861","gazetted":"end of 1859, split in 1863 and 1864","lon":145.7,"lat":-19.8,"dx":-8,"dy":4,"anchor":"end","note":"The Queensland Government needed income, and the pressure to open the Kennedy district was great. Many first runs on the Burdekin date from 1861. Inkerman was taken up by 1862 and was taken over by Robert Towns by mortgage in 1864.","country":"The Gugu Badhun Aboriginal Corporation says Gugu Badhun Country centres on the upper Burdekin River catchment, including the Valley of Lagoons. NQ Dry Tropics acknowledges many groups across the Burdekin region, including the Gugu Badhun, Gudjala, Jangga, Birriah and Widi peoples, without saying which belongs to which district.","src":["atfKennedy","atfIndex","gugubadhun","nqdry"],"flag":"Wallis and Burke’s table gives 1858 for South Kennedy and 1863 for North Kennedy. Their text says the district was first proclaimed at the end of 1859 and had been split in two by 1864. Queensland did not exist before December 1859, so the map uses the text."},{"id":"warrego","name":"Warrego","from":1861,"first":"1861 to 1863","gazetted":"1864","lon":146.3,"lat":-26.2,"dx":-8,"dy":4,"anchor":"end","note":"Joseph Fleming took up more than 100,000 hectares in the Warrego in August 1861 and was declared bankrupt by November 1862. The district was gazetted in 1864. Wallis and Burke say it was not taken up as quickly as districts further east, and that interest became apparent in the 1870s when parties from Melbourne began investing. The Murray–Darling Basin Authority says pastoralists took up land in the 1860s.","country":"The Murray–Darling Basin Authority says the Warrego catchment takes in or closely borders the lands of the Bidjara, Gwamu/Kooma, Gunggari/Kungari, Kunja, Mandandanji, Mardigan and Murrawarri nations.","src":["atfWarrego","atfIndex","mdba"]},{"id":"mitchell","name":"Mitchell","from":1862,"first":"1862","gazetted":"1859","lon":145.25,"lat":-22.9,"dx":-12,"dy":4,"anchor":"end","note":"James Allan and Ernest Davis took up runs in the south-east of the district in early 1862. Landsborough and Buchanan took up Bowen Downs, with finance from the Scottish Australian Investment Company, and its first cattle arrived in October 1862. Lammermoor was taken up in 1863. Wallis and Burke say Aboriginal resistance was defeated less than two decades after the district was opened to white occupation.","country":"Wallis and Burke say Bowen Downs is on the lands of the Iningai people and that Lammermoor is on the lands of the Dalleburra/Yirandali people.","src":["atfMitchell","atfIndex","jcuBowen"],"flag":"The table gives 1859 for the Mitchell district, and the text says it was one of the first districts opened on separation in 1859. The first runs date from 1862, so settlers arrived about three years after the district was named."},{"id":"burke","name":"Burke and the Gulf","label":"Burke","from":1862,"first":"about 1862 to 1864","gazetted":"1865","lon":140.7,"lat":-19,"dx":8,"dy":4,"anchor":"start","note":"John Graham MacDonald brought stock overland to the Nicholson River in 1864 and set up Floraville, near Burketown. The Landsborough River Company had stocked Beamesbrook near Burketown by 1865. Wallis and Burke say run holders had to stock the land before they could apply to lease it, and that the first pastoralists were often backed by finance from the big cities. The district was gazetted in 1865.","country":"The Queensland Government names the Gangalidda and Garawa people for land at Burketown. The sources read for this page do not name the other peoples of the district.","src":["atfBurke","atfIndex","qldGulf"],"flag":"Wallis and Burke say Edward Lamb estimated in 1865 that about 30,000 cattle, 400,000 sheep and 2,000 horses were already in Gregory North and Burke in 1861. That is earlier than the dates of the first named runs, which are 1862 to 1866."},{"id":"gregory","name":"Gregory and the Channel Country","label":"Gregory","from":1866,"first":"1866 or 1868","gazetted":"1873","lon":141.6,"lat":-24.2,"dx":12,"dy":5,"anchor":"start","note":"The first runs in Gregory North date from the mid-1860s, along the Diamantina and then the Georgina. Buckingham Downs was taken up in 1866. In Gregory South, one account gives Nockatunga as the first run in 1866, and another says the first occupation was under a licence of 1868. Both districts were gazetted in 1873. By 1885 all the available land in Gregory South had been claimed.","country":"The Western Rivers Alliance quotes Mithaka Traditional Owners and a woman who calls the Channel Country Wangkangurru/Yarluyandi Country. Other peoples hold Country in these districts, but the sources read for this page do not name them.","src":["atfGregoryN","atfGregoryS","atfIndex","wra"],"flag":"For Gregory South, Wallis and Burke give two accounts. One says the first run was Nockatunga in 1866. The other says the first occupation was under a licence of 1868."}],"flags":["Woodley writes that by the 1880s graziers were moving into the Mitchell, Maranoa and Warrego districts. The district pages used here give first-run dates for those districts in the 1840s and 1860s. Both can be true if “moving into” means filling the districts, but the sources do not say.","The spelling of names differs: Kabi Kabi and Gubbi Gubbi, Gunggari and Kungari or Kogai, and Yirandali and Yirendali. This page uses each source’s own spelling."],"left":["The Cook district in the far north is not on the map. It was gazetted in 1864 (Wallis and Burke), but no source read for this page names whose Country it is.","Coastal and goldfields settlement, sugar and the towns are not shown. The map is about the spread of pastoral runs only."]}};
